241-\section{Introduction}
242:It is well-known that the pure state space $P(\caA)$ of a quantum spin chain $\caA$
243-(UHF-algebra, see subsection \ref{setting})
244:is homogeneous under the action of the asymptotically inner automorphisms \cite{powers}, \cite{brat}, \cite{fkk}.
245-In fact, the homogeneity is proven for much larger class, i.e.,
246-for all the separable simple $C^{*}$-algebras \cite{kos}.
247-In this paper, we focus on the subset $SP(\caA)$ of $P(\caA)$ 
248:consisting of pure states satisfying the split property. (See Definition \ref{split}.)
249:One equivalent condition for a state $\omega\in P(\caA)$ to satisfy the split property is that
250:$\omega$ is quasi-equivalent to $\omega\vert_{\caA_{L}}\otimes \omega\vert_{\caA_{R}}$.
251-(See Remark \ref{splitrem}.)
252-Here, $\omega\vert_{\caA_{L}}$, $\omega\vert_{\caA_{R}}$ are restrictions of $\omega$
--
254-A product state on $\caA=\caA_{L}\otimes \caA_{R}$ has no entanglement between 
255-$\caA_{L}$ and $\caA_{R}$ by definition.
256:In this sense, a  state with the split property has small entanglement between $\caA_{L}$
257-and $\caA_{R}$. Using the result  of  \cite{powers}, \cite{brat}, \cite{fkk},\cite{kos},
258-one can easily see that for any $\omega_{0},\omega_{1}\in SP(\caA)$,
259:there exist asymptotically inner automorphisms $\Xi_{L}$, $\Xi_{R}$ on $\caA_{L}$, $\caA_{R}$
260-such that $\omega_{1}\vert_{\caA_{L}}\sim_{q.e.}\omega_{0}\vert_{\caA_{L}}\circ \Xi_{L}$
261-and $\omega_{1}\vert_{\caA_{R}}\sim_{q.e.}\omega_{0}\vert_{\caA_{R}}\circ \Xi_{R}$.
262:(Here $\sim_{q.e.}$ means quasi-equivalence.)
263:From this and the split property of $\omega_{0}$, $\omega_{1}$, we see that $\omega_{1}$ and
264:$\omega_{0}\circ\lmk \Xi_{L}\otimes \Xi_{R}\rmk$ are quasi-equivalent.
265:The product of automorphisms $\Xi_{L}\otimes \Xi_{R}$ clearly does not
266-create/destroy any entanglement between $\caA_{L}$ and $\caA_{R}$.
267-Hence any $\omega_{0}\in SP(\caA)$
--
270-
271-What we would like to show in this paper is that the situation changes when symmetry comes into the game. This corresponds to the notion of symmetry protected topological phases in physics \cite{ogata}.
272:Let $SPG(\caA)$ be the set of all states in $ SP(\caA)$ which are invariant under the onsite action $\tau$
273:of a finite group $G$. (See Definition \ref{split}.)
274:We now require that  the automorphisms $\Xi_{L}$, $\Xi_{R}$ above to preserve the symmetry
275-i.e., $\Xi_{L}\circ \tau_{L}(g)= \tau_{L}(g)\circ \Xi_{L}$
276-and $\Xi_{R}\circ \tau_{R}(g)= \tau_{R}(g)\circ \Xi_{R}$ for all $g\in G$.
277-(See (\ref{tgg}) for the definition of $\tau_{L}$ and $\tau_{R}$.)
278-For any $\omega_{0},\omega_{1}\in SPG(\caA)$, can we always find such 
279:automorphisms giving $\omega_{1}\sim_{q.e.}\omega_{0}\circ\lmk \Xi_{L}\otimes \Xi_{R}\rmk$?
280-We show that the answer is no in general. 
281:The obstacle is given by the second cohomology class of the projective representation of $G$
282-associated to $\omega\in SPG(\caA)$.
283:We show that this second cohomology class is the complete invariant of this classification.
