585-\paragraph{Two classes of examples}
586:It may be useful to present two concrete cases of our general theorems in the context of standard quantum spin chains with $\ZZ$ or time-reversal symmetry.
587-
588-We consider a quantum spin system on the infinite chain $\bbZ$.
--
590-The system is described by the formal Hamiltonian $H=\sum_{x\in\bbZ}h_x$.
591-The local Hamiltonian $h_x$ acts nontrivially only on sites $y$ such that $|y-x|\le R$, and satisfies $\snorm{h_x}\le B$, where $R$ and $B$ are constants independent of $x$.
592:We assume that each $h_x$ is invariant under the $\ZZ$ transformation given by $(S^{(1)}_x,S^{(2)}_x,S^{(3)}_x)\to(S^{(1)}_x,-S^{(2)}_x,-S^{(3)}_x)$ and $(S^{(1)}_x,S^{(2)}_x,S^{(3)}_x)\to(-S^{(1)}_x,-S^{(2)}_x,S^{(3)}_x)$, or the time-reversal symmetry transformation given by $(S^{(1)}_x,S^{(2)}_x,S^{(3)}_x)\to(-S^{(1)}_x,-S^{(2)}_x,-S^{(3)}_x)$.
593-See section~\ref{s:SR} for details.
594:We also note that any system which is $SO(3)$ invariant is automatically invariant under this $\ZZ$ symmetry. This plays an important role in Appendix~\ref{app:Lie}, where we discuss the cases with compact Lie group symmetry.
595-
596:Let us first assume that the model has translation invariance, i.e., $\bar{S}_x=\bar{S}$ and $h_{x+1}=\tau_1(h_{x})$  for all $x$, where $\tau_1$ is the translation operator.
597-Then the following was conjectured by  Chen, Gu, and Wen \cite{ChenGuWEn2011}, proved for MPS by Watanabe, Po, Vishwanath, and Zaletel \cite{WPVZ}, and was proved by Ogata and Tasaki \cite{OT1}:
598-\begin{C}
599-\label{C:trans}
600:If $\bar{S}$ is a half-odd integer, then it is never the case that the above translation-invariant model (with $\ZZ$ or time-reversal invariance) has a unique gapped ground state.
601-\end{C}
602-In \cite{OT1} the cases for $\ZZ$ symmetry and time-reversal symmetry were treated separately.
603:Here we prove a much more general and unified result, Theorem~\ref{T:trans} below, which corresponds to the original conjecture by Chen, Gu, and Wen \cite{ChenGuWEn2011}.
604-
605-
606:Let us next assume that the model is invariant under the reflection about the origin.
607:We assume the symmetry $\bar{S}_x=\bar{S}_{-x}$ and $h_{-x}=\calR(h_{x})$ for all $x\in\bbZ$, where $\calR$ denotes the reflection map.
608-Then our main result is the following Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type statement.
609-\begin{C}
610-\label{C:ref}
611:If $\bar{S}_0$ is a half-odd integer, then it is never the case that the above reflection-invariant model  (with $\ZZ$ or time-reversal invariance)  has a unique gapped ground state.
612-\end{C}
613:Note that, rather remarkably, the condition for the corollary contains only the spin quantum number $\bar{S}_0$ at the origin; $\bar{S}_x=\bar{S}_{-x}$ on other sites are arbitrary.
614:The above statement is a simple corollary of our general result, Theorem~\ref{T:ref}.
615:The corollary is reminiscent of the well-known fact, often called the Kramers degeneracy, that all the energy eigenvalues are inevitably even-fold degenerate in a system of a single half-odd-integral spin with a $\ZZ$ or time-reversal
616:invariant Hamiltonian.
617-See, e.g., \cite[Chapter 2]{TasakiBook}.
