235:\title{$C^1$-Classification of gapped parent Hamiltonians of quantum spin chains with local symmetry}
236-\author{
237-{\sc Yoshiko Ogata}\footnote{Supported in part by
--
243-
244-\maketitle
245:\begin{abstract}
246-We consider the family of gapped Hamiltonians introduced in~\cite{Fannes:1992vq, Nachtergaele:1996vc} on
247-the quantum spin chain $\bigotimes_{\bbZ}\mn$, {\it with local symmetry} given by a group $G$.
248:The $G$-symmetric gapped Hamiltonians are given by triples $(k,u,V)$, where $u$ is a projective unitary representation of $G$ on a finite dimensional space $\cc^k$,
249-and $V$ is an isometry from $\bbC^k$ to $\cc^n\otimes \cc^k$.
250-We show that Hamiltonians $H_0,H_1$, given by the triples 
251: $(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$ and $(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$ are $C^1$-equivalent if
252:the projective representations $u^{(0)}$ and  $u^{(1)}$ are unitary equivalent.
253:\end{abstract}
254-
255-\section{Introduction}
--
257-\cite{Chen13}
258-\cite{bo}\cite{SW}.
259:In quantum spin systems, we say two gapped Hamiltonians are equivalent if and only if they are connected by a continuous path of uniformly
260:gapped Hamiltonians.  When we further require the path to be $C^1$, we call it $C^1$-classification. It is known that the ground state structure is an invariant of
261:the $C^1$-classification~\cite{Bachmann:2011kw}. The ultimate goal should be classifying all the gapped Hamiltonians in the world.
262:When we impose symmetry, the classification problem raises different mathematical question \cite{Schuch:2011ve}\cite{Chen13}.
263-Two Hamiltonians
264:which are equivalent in the classification without symmetry may be no longer in the same class if we consider the classification {\it with} symmetry. 
265-
266:The general framework of classification with symmetry was considered in \cite{bns}. However, it is in general a hard problem to guarantee the existence of the spectral gap along the path rigorously.
267-As a result, examples of gapped Hamiltonians are quite limited in quantum spin systems whose spatial dimensions are larger than one.
268-However, for one dimensional systems, there is a recipe of gapped Hamiltonians \cite{Fannes:1992vq}.
269:We completely classified Hamiltonians given by this recipe, without breaking translation invariance in \cite{bo}. In \cite{Fannes:1992vq},
270-the existence of the spectral gap is guaranteed by
271-the primitivity of the associated completely positive map. 
272:We carried out the classification, by showing that the space of primitive maps, with an upper bound on Kraus rank, is connected, in \cite{bo}.
273-Recently, an alternative proof  of this fact was introduced in \cite{SW}. Both proof \cite{bo}, and \cite{SW} have their own advantages.
274-The proof in \cite{SW} is simpler and we don't need to care about the detailed structure, i.e., we don't need to know {\it how the primitivity is guaranteed} in details. Because of this simplicity, this proof can provide the analiticity of the path.
275-On the other hand, we find in \cite{Ogata1} and \cite{Ogata2} that the argument in \cite{bo} can be extended to the non-pimitive maps. It also has an advantage in constructing examples, as we know how the primitivity is guaranteed concretely.
276-
277:In this paper, we consider the classification of the class of gapped Hamiltonians, given by the recipe of \cite{Fannes:1992vq}, {\it with} symmetry. The $G$-symmetric gapped Hamiltonians are given by triples $(k,u,V)$, where $u$ is a projective unitary representation of $G$ on a finite dimensional space $\cc^k$,
278-and $V$ is an isometry from $\bbC^k$ to $\cc^n\otimes \cc^k$.
279:This problem is considered in ~\cite{Schuch:2011ve}. The difference here is that we  do not break translation invariance. Mathematically, our task is to guarantee the primitivity of the associated CP-maps, {\it and} the symmetry. 
280-As the recipe in \cite{Fannes:1992vq} cares only about the primitive maps, we can use the method in \cite{SW}. About this problem, \cite{SW} is much stronger method than the one in \cite{bo}.
