  1265	\subsection{SPT-phases}
  1266	We denote the set of all uniformly bounded finite range interactions with
  1267	unique gapped ground state by $\caP_{U.G.}$.
  1268	First, we introduce an equivalence relation $\sim$ in this set $\caP_{U.G.}$
  1269	as follows.:
  1270	\begin{defn}[Definition of $\sim$]\label{simdef}
  1271	Two interactions $\Phi_0,\Phi_1\in \caP_{U.G.}$ are equivalent (denoted as $ \Phi_0\sim\Phi_1$)
  1272	if there is a path 
  1273	$\caP_{U.G.}$
  1274	in $\Phi: [0,1]\to \caP_{U.G.}$
  1275	satisfying the following conditions.
  1276	\begin{description}
  1277	\item[(i)] The path connects given interactions, i.e., $\Phi(0)=\Phi_{0}$ and $\Phi(1)=\Phi_{1}$.
  1278	\item[(ii)] For each $X\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ^{\nu}}$,
  1279	the matrix-valued function $[0,1]\ni s\mapsto \Phi_s(X)\in\caA_{X}$ is smooth,
  1280	\item[(iii)] The gap is uniformly bounded from below by some $\gamma>0$ along the path.
  1281	Namely, for each $s\in[0,1]$, there is a $\gamma_{s}>0$ satisfying (\ref{gapeq1})
  1282	for $\Phi=\Phi(s)$, and $\inf_{s\in [0,1]}\gamma_{s}>0$.
  1283	\item[(iv)] The path of expectation values $[0,1]\ni s\mapsto \omega_s(A)\in\bbC$ of sub-exponentially localized
  1284	elements
  1285	$A\in\caA_{\bbZ^\nu}$ with respect to the ground state $\omega_s$ is regular
  1286	(in the sense that it is differentiable and the derivative is not too large compared to
  1287	some norm of $A$)
  1288	 with respect to $s\in[0,1]$.
  1289	\end{description}
  1290	\end{defn}
  1291	For more precise description of the conditions on the path,  see subsection \ref{autosec} Assumption \ref{assump} Assumption \ref{infautoassump}.
  1292	A part of these assumptions  are for technical reason, namely we would like to apply
  1293	Theorem \ref{autoinf}.
  1294	Physically, this condition means that {\it there is no phase transition along the path}.
  1295	
  1296	Among many interactions in $\caP_{U.G.}$, what corresponds to SPT is
  1297	the ones {\it with short-range entanglement}.
  1298	Recall the definition of on-site interaction (\ref{onsitedef}).
  1299	Fix some on-site interaction $\Phi_{0}$ which belongs to
  1300	$\caP_{U.G.}$.
  1301	Such an interaction can be constructed as follows.
  1302	For each $x\in\bbZ^{\nu}$, fix any unit vector $\xi_{x}\in\bbC^{d}$.
  1303	Let $p_{x}$ be an orthogonal projection on $\bbC^{d}$ onto the subspace
  1304	$\bbC \xi_{x}$.
  1305	Set $\Phi_{0}$ as
  1306	\begin{align*}
  1307	\Phi_{0}\lmk \{x\}\rmk
  1308	=\unit-p_{x},\quad x\in\bbZ^{\nu},
  1309	\end{align*}
  1310	and $\Phi_{0}(X)=0$ for any other $X\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ^{\nu}}$.
  1311	Then $\Phi_{0}\in \caP_{U.G.}$
  1312	and its unique ground state is of the form
  1313	\begin{align*}
  1314	\omega_{\Phi_{0}}=\bigotimes_{x\in\bbZ^{\nu}}\rho_{\xi_{x}},
  1315	\end{align*}
  1316	where $\rho_{\xi_{x}}$ is a vector state on $\Mat_{d}$
  1317	given by $\rho_{\xi_{x}}(A)=\braket{\xi_{x}}{A\xi_{x}}$.
  1318	Note that any on-site interaction can be smoothly deformed into
  1319	an on-site interaction of this form inside of $\caP_{U.G.}$,
  1320	just by smoothly flattering the spectrum of each $\Phi(\{x\})$.
  1321	Furthermore, any on-site interactions of this form can be deformed
  1322	into each other by rotating $\xi_{x}$ smoothly.
  1323	Therefore, the following set does not depend on the choice of $\Phi_{0}$:
  1324	\begin{align*}
  1325	\caP_{U.G.}^{0}:=
  1326	\left\{
  1327	\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.}\mid 
  1328	\Phi\sim \Phi_0.
  1329	\right\}.
  1330	\end{align*}
  1331	As we see in the next subsection, this set physically represents 
  1332	interactions whose unique gapped ground state is {\it short-range entangled}.
  1333	This excludes
  1334	 exotic states with “topological order” (or long-range entanglement) such as the ground states of the Kitaev toric code model \cite{Kitaev}. In general, a pure state with a non-trivial super selection sector
  1335	 has long-range entanglement \cite{NO}.
  1336	
  1337	Now we introduce the on-site  symmetry $\beta$ to the game.
  1338	We are interested in 
  1339	the set 
  1340	\begin{align}\label{pugb}
  1341	\caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^{0}:=
  1342	\left\{
  1343	\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.}^{0}\mid 
  1344	\beta\text{-invariant}
  1345	\right\}.
  1346	\end{align}
  1347	We introduce an equivalence relation $\sim_{\beta}$ on
  1348	this set $\caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^{0}$.
  1349	\begin{defn}[Definition of $\sim_{\beta}$]\label{simbdef}
  1350	Two interactions $\Phi_0,\Phi_1\in  \caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^{0}$ are equivalent (denoted as $ \Phi_0\sim_{\beta}\Phi_1$)
  1351	if there is a path 
  1352	in $\Phi: [0,1]\to \caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^{0}$
  1353	satisfying the conditions (i)-(iv) of Definition \ref{simdef}.
  1354	\end{defn}
  1355	The equivalence class of $ \caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^{0}$
  1356	with this classification are the SPT-phases.
  1357	\begin{defn}[Symmetry Protected Topological (SPT) phases]
  1358	Each equivalence class of ${\caP}_{U.G.{\beta}}^{0}$
  1359	with respect to $\sim_{\beta}$ is called 
  1360	Symmetry Protected Topological (SPT) phases.
  1361	\end{defn}
  1362	This notion of SPT-phases was introduced by Gu and Wen in \cite{GuWen2009}.
  1363	This is the object we would like to classify.
  1364	Analogously, we can define SPT phases $ \caP_{U.G.{{\Theta_{R}}}}^{0}$
  1365	for the reflection symmetry $\Theta_{R}$.
