  1368	\subsection{Split property}
  1369	The split property, studied extensively in operator algebra has turned out to be quite  important 
  1370	in analysis of SPT phases.
  1371	In this section we introduce the  notion.
  1372	
  1373	Let us consider an inclusion of two von Neumann algebras $\caN\subset \caM$.
  1374	We say the inclusion is {\it split}, if there is a type $I$ factor $\caR$
  1375	such that $\caN\subset \caR\subset \caM$.
  1376	The inclusion which shows up in this lecture is the simplest
  1377	case, that is, $\caN$ itself is a type $I$ factor.
  1378	In particular, we will use the following terminology in 
  1379	this lecture.
  1380	\begin{defn}
  1381	Let $\Gamma_1, \Gamma_2 \subset\bbZ^{\nu}$ be non-empty disjoint
  1382	 subsets of $\bbZ^\nu$.
  1383	 A pure state $\omega$ on $\caA_{\Gamma_1\cup\Gamma_2}$
  1384	satisfies the split property with respect to 
  1385	$\Gamma_1$-$\Gamma_2$, if 
  1386	the von Neumann algebra $\pi_\omega(\caA_{\Gamma_1})''$
  1387	is a type $I$ factor.
  1388	\end{defn}
  1389	It is beneficial to heed in mind the following Theorems about type $I$ factors.
  1390	\begin{thm}[Theorem 1.31 V\cite{takesaki}]\label{ubh}
  1391	Let $\caM$ be a type $I$ factor acting on a Hilbert space $\caH$.
  1392	Then there are Hilbert spaces $\caH_1$, $\caH_2$
  1393	and a unitary $U:\caH\to \caH_1\otimes\caH_2$
  1394	such that
  1395	\begin{align*}
  1396	\Ad(U)(\caM)=\caB(\caH_1)\otimes\bbC\unit_{\caH_2}.
  1397	\end{align*}
  1398	\end{thm}
  1399	\begin{thm}[Theorem 2.30 V\cite{takesaki}]
  1400	Let $\caM_1$, $\caM_2$ be von Neumann algebras
  1401	acting on Hilbert spaces $\caH_1$, $\caH_2$.
  1402	Let $\caM$ be a von Neumann 
  1403	algebra acting on $\caH_1\otimes\caH_2$
  1404	generated by the algebraic tensor product $\caM_1{\odot}\caM_2$.
  1405	Then $\caM$ is of type $I$ if and only if $\caM_1$ and $\caM_2$
  1406	are both of type $I$.
  1407	\end{thm}
  1408	Using such properties, we can show the following.
  1409	\begin{prop}\label{splitcharac}
  1410	For a pure state $\omega$ on $\caA_{\Gamma_1\cup\Gamma_2}$, following conditions are equivalent.
  1411	\begin{description}
  1412	\item[(i)] The state $\omega$ satisfies the split property with respect to 
  1413	$\Gamma_1$-$\Gamma_2$
  1414	\item[(ii)] There are states $\omega_1$, $\omega_2$
  1415	on $\caA_{\Gamma_1}$, $\caA_{\Gamma_2}$
  1416	such that $\omega$ and $\omega_1\otimes\omega_2$ are quasi-equivalent.
  1417	\item[(iii)] There are irreducible representations
  1418	$(\caH_1,\pi_1)$, $(\caH_2,\pi_2)$ of $\caA_{\Gamma_1}$,
  1419	$\caA_{\Gamma_2}$
  1420	and a unit vector $\Omega\in\caH_1\otimes\caH_2$
  1421	such that $(\caH_1\otimes\caH_2,\pi_1\otimes\pi_2, \Omega)$
  1422	is a GNS-triple of $\omega$.
  1423	\end{description}
  1424	\end{prop}
  1425	Recalling the ``physical" interpretation that ``two states are quasi-equivalent means they are macroscopically the same", the above proposition tells us that
  1426	$\omega$ satisfies the split property with respect to 
  1427	$\Gamma_1$-$\Gamma_2$
  1428	if it is macroscopically product state with respect to the $\Gamma_1$-$\Gamma_2$ cut.
