  1458	\subsection{Automorphic equivalence}\label{autosec}
  1459	For the investigation of the equivalence relation $\sim$, automorphic equivalence
  1460	on smooth path of interactions plays an important role.
  1461	Automorphic equivalence started as Hasting's adiabatic Lemma \cite{h1}\cite{hw}.
  1462	After seminal mathematical development in \cite{bmns} \cite{nsy},
  1463	it is now a strong mathematical machinery to analyze 
  1464	gapped state phases.
  1465	It basically says the following :  given a smooth path of gapped local Hamiltonians,
  1466	there is a {\it concrete} smooth path of automorphisms
  1467	interpolating ground state spaces.
  1468	Original Hastings's adiabatic Lemma was about finite systems.
  1469	In the framework given by \cite{bmns} \cite{nsy},
  1470	 the existence of gap for {\it  finite} systems is assumed as well, and 
  1471	the thermodynamic limit taken afterwords.
  1472	An infinite version, assuming the gap in the {\it infinite} system
  1473	was derived in \cite{mo}.
  1474	
  1475	In this subsection, we review  Hasting's original adiabatic Lemma,
  1476	and introduce its infinite dimensional version.
  1477	Let 
  1478	\[
  1479	H: [0,1]\ni s\to H(s)\in\Mat_n
  1480	\]
  1481	be a smooth path of self-adjoint matrices of size $n$.
  1482	In physical settings this $H(s)$ is a Hamiltonian.
  1483	We consider the situation that there is a gap
  1484	between the lowest eigenvalue of $H(s)$
  1485	and the rest of the spectrum, which is uniform in $s$.
  1486	What we are interested in is the lowest eigenvalue eigenspace
  1487	of the Hamiltonian i.e, the ground state space.
  1488	For each parameter $s\in [0,1]$,
  1489	let $E(s)$ be the lowest eigenvalue of $H(s)$
  1490	and $P(s)$ be the spectral projection of $H(s)$
  1491	corresponding to the eigenvalue $E(s)$.
  1492	As we saw in subsection \ref{quantumspinsec},
  1493	a ground state of $H(s)$
  1494	is a positive linear functional on $\Mat_n$
  1495	with support under $P(s)$.

