  2158	\subsection{$H^{2}(G,\Uo)$ and projective representations}\label{onsiteone}
  2159	
  2160	
  2161	
  2162	
  2163	For SPT-phases in one dimension with on-site symmetry,
  2164	physicists conjectured that there is a $H^{2}(G,\Uo)$-valued invariant
  2165	of the classification.
  2166	In this subsection, we recall the definition of $H^{2}(G,\Uo)$ and its relation to projective representations.
  2167	
  2168	A $2$-cochain $C^2(G,\Uo)$ is a map from 
  2169	$G^{\times 2}$ to $\Uo$.
  2170	It is said to be a $2$-cocycle if the relation 
  2171	\begin{align*}
  2172	\sigma(gh,k) \sigma(g,h) 
  2173	=
  2174	\sigma(g,hk) \sigma(h,k)
  2175	\end{align*}
  2176	holds.
  2177	The set of all $2$-cocycles are denoted by $Z^2(G,\Uo)$.
  2178	We introduce an equivalence relation to $Z^2(G,\Uo)$ by
  2179	$\sigma'\simeq \sigma$ if there exists $c:G\mapsto \bbT$ such that
  2180	\begin{align*}
  2181	\sigma'(g,h)=
  2182	\sigma(g,h) \frac{c(gh) }{ c(g)  c(h) }.
  2183	\end{align*}
  2184	The equivalence classes forms a group, which is the second group cohomology 
  2185	$H^2(G,{U(1)})$.
  2186	
  2187	The second group cohomology $H^2(G,{U(1)})$
  2188	shows up naturally, when we consider projective representations.
  2189	A projective representation of $G$ on a Hilbert space $\caH$
  2190	is a map from $G$ to a unitary operators $\caU(\caH)$
  2191	on $\caH$
  2192	satisfying
  2193	\begin{align*}
