  2270	\subsection{Split property of unique gapped ground state in $1$-d}
  2271	
  2272	An important property of the unique gapped ground state in one dimension
  2273	is the split property.
  2274	Using this property, one can easily define indices, which turns out to be invariants of the classification.
  2275	More precisely, the following Theorem is crucial in order to define the SPT-index
  2276	in one-dimensional systems. 
  2277	\begin{thm}[Matsui '13]\label{matsuisplit}
  2278	Let $\omega_{\Phi}$ be a {\it  unique gapped $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state}
  2279	for $\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.}$.
  2280	% and denote by  $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ the corresponding bulk Hamiltonian. 
  2281	%Assume that $0$ is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ and
  2282	%there exists $\gamma>0$ such that $\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})\setminus\{0\}\subset [\gamma,\infty)$.
  2283	Then $\omega_{\Phi}$ satisfies the split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$, i.e.,
  2284	$\pi_{\omega_{\Phi, R}}(\caA_R)''$ is a type I factor.
  2285	\end{thm}
  2286	Thanks to this theorem, known for {\it any} unique gapped ground state, 
  2287	we can classify
  2288	\begin{align*}
  2289	\caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}:=
  2290	\left\{
  2291	\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.}\mid 
  2292	\beta\text{-invariant}
  2293	\right\}.
  2294	\end{align*}
  2295	not only the SPT-phases $\caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}^0$ (\ref{pugb}).
  2296	Although it is expected that $\caP_{U.G.}^0= \caP_{U.G.}$ for one dimensional systems,
  2297	there is no result proving it.
  2298	Therefore, at least at this stage of research,
  2299	classification of $\caP_{U.G.}$ is stronger result than
  2300	classification of $\caP_{U.G.}^0$.

