  2971	\subsection{Lieb-Schultz-Mattis (LSM) type theorems}
  2972	The cohomology valued index $h_{\Phi,\beta}$ is important as an invariant of our classification of SPT phases.
  2973	However, there is still another use of it, that is, Lieb-Schultz-Mattis (LSM) type theorem which we explain in this subsection.
  2974	%In general,  Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorem states that  under certain condition including some symmetry, there is no interactions with unique gapped ground state.
  2975	
  2976	It is a hard problem to decide if a given interaction has a unique gapped ground state or not.
  2977	For example, Haldane conjectured in 1983 that
  2978	Antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain  
  2979	\begin{align*}
  2980	\Phi_{Heisenberg}(\{x,x+1\})=\sum_{j=1}^3\,S^{(x)}_jS^{(x+1)}_j,
  2981	\end{align*}
  2982	does not have a unique gapped ground state if $d$ is {even},
  2983	while it does if $d$ is {odd}.
  2984	The latter part of the conjecture is still an open problem.
  2985	Lieb-Schultz-Mattis \cite{LSM} and  Affleck-Lieb \cite{AL}
  2986	 proved affirmatively the first part of the conjecture.
  2987	They proved the following : if interaction has 
  2988	$\Uo$ on-site symmetry  and space translation symmetry 
  2989	and the spin-size $d$ is even, then it cannot have a unique gapped ground state.
  2990	They used the continuity of $\Uo$ in an ingenious way, to prove the existence of
  2991	low-energy excitation.
  2992	There has been a lot of progress after that \cite{HL}\cite{Os}\cite{NS}\cite{Matsui2}, but
  2993	traditionally, the {symmetry} has been some {continuous} ones.
  2994	Recently, it was claimed by physicists that Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorem holds for some 
  2995	{discrete} symmetries. It is proved for matrix product states parent Hamiltonians
  2996	\cite{ChenGuWEn2011},
  2997	\cite{WPVZ}
  2998	\cite{fuji}.
  2999	
  3000	Using our $H^2(G,\Uo)$-valued index, we can prove their claim.
  3001	Let $G$ be a finite group and let $\beta_g:=\bigotimes_{\bbZ}\Ad(U_g)$
  3002	with $U$ a projective representation of $G$
  3003	on $\bbC^d$.
  3004	\begin{thm}[\cite{ot}, \cite{OTT}]
  3005	If the second cohomology class $h$ of $U$ is non-trivial, then there is no
  3006	translation invariant interaction in $\caP_{U.G.\beta}$.
  3007	\end{thm}
  3008	\begin{proof}
  3009	Suppose that there exists an interaction $\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.\beta}$ which is translation invariant.
  3010	Let 
  3011	$h_{\Phi,\beta}^x\in H^2(G, U(1))$ be an
  3012	 index associated to $\left.\omega_{\Phi}\right\vert_{\caA_{[x,\infty)\cap \bbZ}}$
  3013	 for each $x\in\bbZ$. (Note that we can cut the chain anywhere.)
  3014	Then we have
  3015	\[
  3016	h_{\Phi,\beta}^ {x}=h+h_{\Phi,\beta}^ {x+1},\quad\text{and}\quad
  3017	h_{\Phi,\beta}^ {x}=h_{\Phi,\beta}^{x+1}.
  3018	\]
  3019	Hence $h=0$.
  3020	\end{proof}
  3021	Recall that antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain $\Phi_{Heisenberg}$
  3022	has the $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$-symmetry.
  3023	There, cohomology class of the projective representation $U$ of $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$ is trivial if $d$ is odd,
  3024	nontrivial if $d$ is even.
  3025	Hence we have $\Phi_{Heisenberg}\notin \caP_{U.G.\beta}$, if $d$ is even.
  3026	This gives an alternative proof for 
  3027	the first half of Haldane conjecture.
  3028	Analogously, we can prove the following, using the $H^2(G,\Uo)$-index.
  3029	\begin{thm}[\cite{OTT}]
  3030	If there is $\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.\beta}$, origin-reflection invariant, 
  3031	then $h=2 h_1$ with some $h_1\in H^2(G,\Uo)$.
  3032	\end{thm}
  3033	
  3034	
  3035	\subsection{A classification of pure states on quantum spin chains satisfying the 
  3036	split property with on-site finite group symmetries}
  3037	
  3038	We obtained an invariant of the classification of SPT phases for
  3039	on-site finite group symmetry,
  3040	It is an open problem if they are {\it complete invariant}.
  3041	
  3042	However, for on-site symmetry, we still can say that it is
  3043	a complete invariant of some (philosophically)
  3044	analogous classification.
  3045	That is, a classification of pure states on quantum spin chains satisfying the 
  3046	split property with on-site finite group symmetries,
  3047	
  3048	The classification we consider here is the following.
  3049	\begin{defn}[\cite{GS}]
  3050	Let $\omega_{0}$, $\omega_{1}$ be pure $\beta$-invariant states on $\caA_{\bbZ}$
  3051	satisfying the split property with respect to $\caA_{L}$, $\caA_{R}$.\\
  3052	We write $\omega_{0}\sim_{{\rm split},\beta}\omega_{1}$
  3053	if there exist automorphisms $\alpha_{L}$ 
  3054	and $\alpha_{R}$ on $\caA_L$, $\caA_R$ such that
  3055	\begin{description}
  3056	\item[(i)]
  3057	$\omega_{1}$ and $\omega_{0}\circ\lmk\alpha_{L}\otimes \alpha_{R}\rmk$
  3058	are quasi-equivalent,
  3059	\item[(ii)]
  3060	$\alpha_L\circ\beta_{g,L}=\beta_{g,L}\circ \alpha_L$, and
  3061	$\alpha_R\circ\beta_{g,R}=\beta_{g,R}\circ \alpha_R$
  3062	for all $g\in G$.
  3063	\end{description}
  3064	\end{defn}
  3065	How can this be (philosophically)
  3066	analogous to our original classification?
  3067	The interpretation comes from rough identification
  3068	that 
  3069	\begin{center}
  3070	gapped \\
  3071	$\approx$  ground state is almost product with respect to
  3072	left-right cut\\
  3073	 $\approx$
  3074	 ground state has the split property.
  3075	\end{center}
  3076	These $\approx$ are not $=$.
  3077	However, philosophically, they share the concept.
  3078	From this analogy, {\it the existence of smooth path of 
  3079	interactions with unique gapped ground state}
  3080	is rephrased as {\it  the existence of
  3081	 a map which does not change the correlation
  3082	between left and right chains}.
  3083	In (i) of the classification, we are considering an automorphism
  3084	$\alpha_{L}\otimes \alpha_{R}$
  3085	of product form. This is a kind of map which does not change 
  3086	the correlation
  3087	between left and right chains at all.
  3088	Hence, we regard this (i) as an analog of $\sim$.
  3089	With this interpretation, adding the condition (ii)
  3090	corresponds to $\sim_\beta$, that is, the requirement
  3091	the symmetry should be preserved along the path.
  3092	
  3093	Note that the $H^2(G,\Uo)$-valued index is actually defined
  3094	not only for $\omega_{\Phi}$ with $\beta$-invariant $\Phi\in\caP_{U.G.}$,
  3095	but it is also defined
  3096	for a $\beta$-invariant pure split state $\omega$.
  3097	Let us denote that $H^2(G,\Uo)$-valued index by $h(\omega)$,
  3098	for $\beta$-invariant pure split state $\omega$.
  3099	
  3100	Then we obtain the following.
  3101	\begin{thm}\cite{GS}\label{geometry}
  3102	The $H^2(G,\Uo)$-valued index is a complete invariant of
  3103	$\sim_{{\rm split},\beta}$.
  3104	\end{thm}
  3105	Homogeneity of pure state space of separable simple $C^*$-algebras
  3106	has been studied extensively from old time in the operator algebra community
  3107	\cite{powers}, \cite{brat}, \cite{fkk}, \cite{kos}.
  3108	For the proof of Theorem \ref{geometry},
  3109	we used the machinery developed in these papers.
  3110	The point of our analysis, compared to theirs is how to encode the $H^2(G,\Uo)$

