254:\begin{abstract}
255:We introduce a $\bbZ_2$-index for time reversal invariant Hamiltonians with unique gapped ground state on quantum spin chains. 
256:We show this is an invariant of a $C^1$-classification of gapped Hamiltonians.
257-Analogous results hold
258:for more general on-site finite group symmetry, 
259-with the 2-cohomology class
260:as the invariant. 
261:\end{abstract}
262-
263-\section{Introduction}
264-The notion of symmetry protected topological (SPT) phases was introduced by Gu and Wen [GW].
265-It is defined as follows:
266-we consider the set of all Hamiltonians with some symmetry, 
267:which have a unique gapped ground state in the bulk.
268-We regard two of such Hamiltonians are equivalent, if
269-there is a continuous path inside that family, connecting them.
270-By this equivalence relation, we may classify the Hamiltonians 
271-in this family.
272:A Hamiltonian which has only on-site interaction can be regarded as 
273-a trivial one. The set of Hamiltonians equivalent to such trivial ones represents a
274-trivial phase.
275-If a phase is nontrivial, it is a SPT phase.
276-A typical nontrivial example of an SPT phase is the Haldane phase \cite{Haldane1983a}\cite{Haldane1983b} in quantum spin chains with odd integer spin. 
277-Whether the Haldane phase is SPT or not has been studied substantially and
278-produced a fruitful theory of SPT phase 
279-\cite{Affleck:1988vr},
280-\cite{denNijsRommelse},\cite{Kennedy1990}
281-\cite{kt},\cite{kt2},\cite{Perez-Garcia2008},\cite{GuWen2009}, 
282-\cite{po},\cite{po2}, \cite{ChenGuWEn2011}.
283-
284:A natural question to ask is what are invariants of this classification.
285-Following an earlier attempt in [PWSVC], 
286:Pollmann, Turner, Berg, and Oshikawa [PTBO1,PTBO2] introduced $\bbZ_2$-indices for injective matrix products states which have either $\bbZ_2\times \bbZ_2$ on-site symmetry(dihedral group of $\pi$-rotations about $x$, $y$, and $z$-axes), reflection symmetry, or time reversal symmetry.  
287:The $\bbZ_2$-index beyond the framework of matrix product state was recently introduced by Tasaki
288:for systems satisfying on-site $U(1)$-symmetry together with one of $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$-onsite symmetry/reflection symmetry/time reversal symmetry \cite{ta}.
289:He showed that these are actually invariant of the classification.
290-In \cite{bn}, an operator called {\it excess spin} was introduced for two one-dimensional models with continuous symmetry,
291:and was shown to be related to the classification of gapped Hamiltonians on the half infinite chain.
292-
293:In this paper, we focus on SPT phases of quantum spin chains in the bulk, with the time reversal symmetry.
294:We introduce a $\bbZ_2$-index for the time reversal invariant Hamiltonians with unique gapped ground state. The key ingredient is the projective representation associated to the unique bulk gapped ground state.
295:As the time reversal symmetry is discrete and anti-linear, something like excess spin looks hard to define. However, by considering the associated projective representation, we may define the $\bbZ_2$-index.
296:It turns out that this $\bbZ_2$-index is an invariant of the $C^1$-classification: suppose that
297-there is a $C^1$-path of interactions, and suppose that if we associate some suitable boundary condition,
298:it gives local Hamiltonians which are gapped for an
299-increasing sequence of finite boxes. (See Definition \ref{boundary}.)
300:Then the $\bbZ_2$-index does not change along the path.
301-These boundary conditions can be arbitrary, as long as they guarantee the gap. We may take it as periodic boundary condition, for example.
302:Furthermore, the boundary condition itself does not need to be time reversal invariant.
303:As stated above, for time reversal invariant injective matrix product state,
304:a $\bbZ_2$-index was introduced in \cite{po}.
305:It turns out that this is a special case of our $\bbZ_2$-index.
306-
307:{\new { The key ingredient for the definition of the index is the {\it split property} that is satisfied by unique gapped ground states.
308:This important property of unique gapped ground states was proven by T. Matsui in \cite{Matsui2}.
309-Unfortunately, this significant result  has not been paid enough attention to so far 
310-(up to our recent paper \cite{ot} on  Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorems), because
311-it looked to have no new physical application. 
312-In this paper, we would like to emphasize that it {\it does}
313-have a very important application to a major question in physics, namely, to the classification problem of SPT phases.
314:The use of split property for classification problem is one important new suggestion from this paper, which turns out to be a very strong tool.
315-}}
316-
317-Analogous argument can be carried out
318:for on-site finite group symmetry, in particular the $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$-symmetry,
319:and the cohomology classs of projective representation 
320:is an invariant along the analogous path of such Hamiltonians. (See Appendix \ref{onsite}.)
321:The projective representation for on-site symmetry has been known for some time. In particular,  Matsui developed a mathematical theory for quantum spin chains based on projective representations of on-site group symmetry, for general pure states which satisfy the split property \cite{Matsui1}. 
322-
323-
324-{\new {
325:What is new in this paper is identifying the 2-cohomology of the projective representation
326:as the index of SPT-phases, and showing that it is actually an invariant of the classification.
327:The projective representation shows up as follows.
328:Let us consider the unique gapped ground state
329-$\omega$ of an interaction satisfying the symmetry.
330-We consider the GNS triple 
331-$(\caH_R,\pi_R,\Omega_R)$
332-of the restriction $\left. \omega\right\vert_{\caA_R}$ of $\omega$ to the right infinite chain $\caA_R$.
333:The split property of $\omega$ is by definition that $\pi_R(\caA_R)''$
334-is $*$-isomorphic to $B(\caK)$, for some Hilbert space $\caK$.
335-By this $*$-isomorphism, the action of the symmetry on $\pi_R(\caA_R)''$
336-(which exists because of the invariance of $\omega$ under the symmetry) can be translated to
337-the action on $B(\caK)$.
338-However, by the Wigner Theorem, the action on $B(\caK)$ can be given by
339-unitary/ anti-unitary.
340:This gives the projective representation associated to $\omega$.
341:And our index is the 2-cohomology associated to this projective representation.
342-
343-But how can one prove the stability of such objects like second cohomology class?
344:The index shows up rather abstractly in the context of the GNS
345-representation. In particular, it is not an observable.
346-Our idea is to carry out the quasi-equivalence argument
347-combined with the factorization property of quasi-local automorphisms.
348-More precisely, it is known \cite{bmns} that if $\Phi_0$ and $\Phi_1$ are interactions
349:connected by the path of gapped Hamiltonians, then there is
350-a quasi-local automorphism $\alpha$
351:which connects the corresponding unique gapped ground states as
352-$\omega_{\Phi_1}=\omega_{\Phi_0}\circ\alpha$.
353-We show that this $\alpha$ satisfies a factorization property, namely,
354-there are automorphisms $\alpha_R$ on $\caA_R$
355-and  $\alpha_L$ on $\caA_L$
356-such that $\alpha\circ \lmk \alpha_L^{-1}\otimes \alpha_R^{-1}\rmk$
357-is inner.
358-(When system satisfies the symmetry, $\alpha_R$ can be taken 
359-to commute with (anti-)automorphisms implementing the symmetry.)
360:From this and the split property,
361-we show that $\left.{\omega_{\Phi_0}}\right\vert_{\caA_R}\circ\alpha_R$
362-and ${\omega_{\Phi_1}}_{\caA_R}$
363-are quasi-equivalent.
364-This simple observation turns out to be the key for our analysis.
365-Using the $*$-isomorphism coming from this quasi-equivalence
366:and the $*$-isomorphisms coming from the split property
367-of $\omega_{\Phi_0}$, $\omega_{\Phi_1}$, we can prove the
368:stability of the index. (See section \ref{split}.)
369:Although the automorphic equivalence is a well-developed subject, 
370-we believe
371-the use of it combined with quasi-equivalence like this,
372-cannot be found in the literatures. This is a new technical suggestion from this paper, which is simple,
373-but turns out to be very useful.
