410-The physical impact of our result is as follows.
411:In a word, we here develop a mathematical index theorem, with which the observations in the physics literature such as [CG,PTOB1,PTOB2,CGW] about the indices and phase structures in quantum spin chains with time reversal 
412:%or $\ZZ$ on-site
413-symmetry are 
414-made rigorous.
415-To see an important example, consider $S=1$ quantum spin chains with the AKLT interaction [AKLT]
416-\begin{equation}
417-\Phi_{\rm AKLT}(X)=
418-\begin{cases}
419-\sum_{\nu=1}^3S^\nu_jS^\nu_{j+1}+(\sum_{\nu=1}^3S^\nu_jS^\nu_{j+1})^2/3&\text{if $X=\{j,j+1\}$ with $j\in\bbZ$}\\
420-0&\text{otherwise},
421-\end{cases}
422-\end{equation}
423:and the trivial on-site intereaction
424-\begin{equation}
425-\Phi_{\rm trivial}(X)=
426-\begin{cases}
427-(S^3_j)^2&\text{if $X=\{j\}$ with $j\in\bbZ$}\\
428-0&\text{otherwise}.
429-\end{cases}
430-\end{equation}
431:See section \ref{index} for notations.
432:Both of them are time reversal invariant.
433:It is known that these models have a unique gapped ground state, which we denote by $\varphi_{\rm AKLT}$ and $\varphi_{\rm trivial}$. Both of $\varphi_{\rm AKLT}$ and $\varphi_{\rm trivial}$
434-are matrix product states.
435-{\new{What is believed in physics community is that 
436:AKLT and trivial interaction should belong to different phases, if we require time reversal symmetry
437-to be preserved.
438-For example, if we interpolate them as $s\Phi_{\rm AKLT}+(1-s)\Phi_{\rm trivial}$, with an interpolation parameter
439-$s\in[0,1]$, it is expected that there is a point $s\in (0,1)$
440:that the interaction does not have a unique gapped ground state.}}
441:Our theorem, along with results in [PTOB1,PTOB2,CGW,Tas2] about matrix product states, shows that these ground states are characterized by the indices $\sigma_{\varphi_{\rm AKLT}}=-1$ and $\sigma_{\varphi_{\rm trivial}}=1$.(See section \ref{oshikawa}, for our index in matrix product states.)
442:%(This is true when time reversal symmetry or on-site $\ZZ$ symmetry is used.)
443-We then get the following.
444-\begin{cor}
445:The two interactions $\Phi_{\rm AKLT}$ and $\Phi_{\rm trivial}$ can never be connected by a $C^1$-path of time reversal invariant 
446:%or $\ZZ$ invariant 
447-interactions satisfying the  {\it Condition B}.
448-\end{cor}
449-See section \ref{c1main} for the definition of {\it Condition B}.
450-Analogous result can be shown for $\ZZ$-symmetry.
451:This roughly means that there must be a gapless model in between the models with $\Phi_{\rm AKLT}$ and $\Phi_{\rm trivial}$, provided that the time reversal or $\ZZ$ on-site symmetry is preserved.\footnote{%
452-We show that, for any $C^1$-path of interactions with the required symmetry and for any $C^1$-path of boundary conditions without symmetry, there must be a point at which the energy gap of a finite chain vanishes as the length of the chain increases.
453-}
454:The conjuecture that the AKLT model is in a nontrivial SPT phase has been established for the cases with time reversal symmetry and on-site $\ZZ$ symmetry.
455:Note that [Tas1] proves similar result, but with extra assumption that the interactions are also U(1) invariant.
456-However, his path does not need to be $C^1$.
457:%Similar results are expected for models with reflection invariant interactions [PTOB1,PTOB2,CGW,Tas1,Tas2], but the present method does not apply to such models.
