333:\noindent{\bf Gapped ground state phases.}
334:Now we introduce the $C^1$-classification 
335:of gapped Hamiltonians. 
336-We say  $\Phi:[0,1]\ni t\mapsto\Phi(t)\in  {\caJ}$ 
337:is a continuous and piecewise $C^1$-path if  for each $X\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$,
338:$[0,1]\ni t\mapsto \Phi(t;X)\in {\caA}_X$ is continuous and piecewise $C^1$
339-with respect to the norm topology.
340:\begin{defn}[$C^1$-classification of gapped Hamiltonians]\label{def:phasec}
341:Let $H_0,H_1$ be gapped  Hamiltonians associated with interactions
342-$\Phi_{0},\Phi_{1}\in{\caJ}$.
343-We say that  $H_0,H_1$ are 
344:$C^1$-equivalent if the following conditions are satisfied.
345-\begin{enumerate}
346:\item There exists $m\in\nan$ and a continuous and piecewise $C^1$-path $\Phi:[0,1]\to {\caJ}_m$ such that $\Phi(0)=\Phi_0$, 
347-$\Phi(1)= \Phi_1$.
348-\item  Let   $H(t)$ be the Hamiltonian associated with $\Phi(t)$ for each $t\in[0,1]$.
349-There are $\gamma>0$, $N_0\in\nan$, and finite intervals $I(t)=[a(t), b(t)]$, whose endpoints $a(t), b(t)$ smoothly depending on $t\in[0,1]$,
350-such that for all finite intervals $\Lambda\subset\bbZ$ with $|\Lambda|\ge N_0$,
351-the smallest eigenvalue of $H(t)_\Lambda$ is in $I(t)$ and
352-the rest of the spectrum is in $[b(t)+\gamma,\infty)$.
353-\end{enumerate}
354-%
355-\end{defn}
356:The advantage of considering $C^1$-paths over just continuous ones is
357-as follows.
358-The following theorem is a special case of the Theorem 5.5 of ~\cite{Bachmann:2011kw}.
359-\begin{thm}[\cite{Bachmann:2011kw}]\label{qla}
360:Suppose that two gapped Hamiltonians $H_0,H_1$ are 
361:$C^1$-equivalent.
362-Then, for $\Gamma=(-\infty,-1]\cap \bbZ$, $\Gamma=[0,\infty)\cap\bbZ$ and $\Gamma=\bbZ$, there exists a quasi-local automorphism $\alpha_\Gamma$ of $\caA_\Gamma$ such that
363-\begin{equation*}
364-\caS_\Gamma(H_1) = \caS_\Gamma(H_0) \circ \alpha_\Gamma.
365-\end{equation*}
366-\end{thm}
367:See ~\cite{Bachmann:2011kw} for the general statement and the definition of quasi-locality. In other words, the structure of the bulk and of the left/right edge ground state spaces are invariants of the $C^1$-classification.
