   235	\title{$C^1$-Classification of gapped parent Hamiltonians of quantum spin chains with local symmetry}
   236	\author{
   237	{\sc Yoshiko Ogata}\footnote{Supported in part by
   238	the Grants-in-Aid for
   239	Scientific Research, JSPS.}\\
   240	{\small Graduate School of Mathematical Sciences}\\
   241	{\small The University of Tokyo, Komaba, Tokyo, 153-8914, Japan}
   242	}
   243	
   244	\maketitle
   245	\begin{abstract}
   246	We consider the family of gapped Hamiltonians introduced in~\cite{Fannes:1992vq, Nachtergaele:1996vc} on
   247	the quantum spin chain $\bigotimes_{\bbZ}\mn$, {\it with local symmetry} given by a group $G$.
   248	The $G$-symmetric gapped Hamiltonians are given by triples $(k,u,V)$, where $u$ is a projective unitary representation of $G$ on a finite dimensional space $\cc^k$,
   249	and $V$ is an isometry from $\bbC^k$ to $\cc^n\otimes \cc^k$.
   250	We show that Hamiltonians $H_0,H_1$, given by the triples 
   251	 $(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$ and $(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$ are $C^1$-equivalent if
   252	the projective representations $u^{(0)}$ and  $u^{(1)}$ are unitary equivalent.
   253	\end{abstract}
   254	
   255	\section{Introduction}
   256	Recently, gapped ground state phases attract a lot of attentions \cite{Chen:2010gb}\cite {Chen:2011iq}\cite {Schuch:2011ve}\cite{Bachmann:2011kw} 
   257	\cite{Chen13}
   258	\cite{bo}\cite{SW}.
   259	In quantum spin systems, we say two gapped Hamiltonians are equivalent if and only if they are connected by a continuous path of uniformly
   260	gapped Hamiltonians.  When we further require the path to be $C^1$, we call it $C^1$-classification. It is known that the ground state structure is an invariant of
   261	the $C^1$-classification~\cite{Bachmann:2011kw}. The ultimate goal should be classifying all the gapped Hamiltonians in the world.
   262	When we impose symmetry, the classification problem raises different mathematical question \cite{Schuch:2011ve}\cite{Chen13}.
   263	Two Hamiltonians
   264	which are equivalent in the classification without symmetry may be no longer in the same class if we consider the classification {\it with} symmetry. 
   265	
   266	The general framework of classification with symmetry was considered in \cite{bns}. However, it is in general a hard problem to guarantee the existence of the spectral gap along the path rigorously.
   267	As a result, examples of gapped Hamiltonians are quite limited in quantum spin systems whose spatial dimensions are larger than one.
   268	However, for one dimensional systems, there is a recipe of gapped Hamiltonians \cite{Fannes:1992vq}.
   269	We completely classified Hamiltonians given by this recipe, without breaking translation invariance in \cite{bo}. In \cite{Fannes:1992vq},
   270	the existence of the spectral gap is guaranteed by
   271	the primitivity of the associated completely positive map. 
   272	We carried out the classification, by showing that the space of primitive maps, with an upper bound on Kraus rank, is connected, in \cite{bo}.
   273	Recently, an alternative proof  of this fact was introduced in \cite{SW}. Both proof \cite{bo}, and \cite{SW} have their own advantages.
   274	The proof in \cite{SW} is simpler and we don't need to care about the detailed structure, i.e., we don't need to know {\it how the primitivity is guaranteed} in details. Because of this simplicity, this proof can provide the analiticity of the path.
   275	On the other hand, we find in \cite{Ogata1} and \cite{Ogata2} that the argument in \cite{bo} can be extended to the non-pimitive maps. It also has an advantage in constructing examples, as we know how the primitivity is guaranteed concretely.
   276	
   277	In this paper, we consider the classification of the class of gapped Hamiltonians, given by the recipe of \cite{Fannes:1992vq}, {\it with} symmetry. The $G$-symmetric gapped Hamiltonians are given by triples $(k,u,V)$, where $u$ is a projective unitary representation of $G$ on a finite dimensional space $\cc^k$,
   278	and $V$ is an isometry from $\bbC^k$ to $\cc^n\otimes \cc^k$.
   279	This problem is considered in ~\cite{Schuch:2011ve}. The difference here is that we  do not break translation invariance. Mathematically, our task is to guarantee the primitivity of the associated CP-maps, {\it and} the symmetry. 
   280	As the recipe in \cite{Fannes:1992vq} cares only about the primitive maps, we can use the method in \cite{SW}. About this problem, \cite{SW} is much stronger method than the one in \cite{bo}.
   281	 
   282	Now let us state our result in detail. See Appendix \ref{sec:nota} for notations.
   283	For $\bbN\ni n\geq 2$, let $\caA$ be the finite dimensional C*-algebra $\caA = \Mat_n$, the algebra of $n\times n$ matrices. 
   284	Throughout this article, this $n$ is fixed as the dimension of the spin under consideration, and we fix an orthonormal basis $\{\psi_\mu\}_{\mu=1}^n$ of $\cc^n$.
   285	We denote the set of all finite subsets in ${\bbZ}$ by ${\mathfrak S}$.
   286	The number of elements in a finite set $\Lambda\subset {\bbZ}$ is denoted by
   287	$|\Lambda|$.
   288	When we talk about intervals in $\bbZ$, $[a,b]$ for $a\le b$,
   289	means the interval in $\bbZ$, i.e., $[a,b]\cap \bbZ$.
   290	We denote the set of all finite intervals 
   360	if there exists $\gamma>0$ and $N_0\in\nan$ 
   361	such that the difference 
   362	between the smallest and the next-smallest eigenvalue of $H_\Lambda$, is bounded below by 
   363	$\gamma$,  for all finite intervals $\Lambda\subset\bbZ$ with $|\Lambda|\ge N_0$. 
   364	\end{defn}
   365	In this definition, the smallest eigenvalue can be degenerated in general.
   366	
   367	
   368	Now we introduce the $C^1$-classification of gapped Hamiltonians with $\beta_U$-symmetry.
   369	We say  $\Phi:[0,1]\ni t\mapsto\Phi(t)\in  {\caJ}$ 
   370	is a continuous and piecewise $C^1$-path if  for each $X\in{\mathfrak S}$, the path
   371	$[0,1]\ni t\mapsto \Phi(t;X)\in {\caA}_X$ is continuous and piecewise $C^1$
   372	with respect to the norm topology.
   373	\begin{defn}[$C^1$-classification of gapped Hamiltonians with symmetry]\label{def:phasec}
   374	Let $U:G\to \caU_n$ be a unitary representation of a group $G$ on $\bbC^n$.
   375	Let $H_0,H_1$ be gapped  Hamiltonians associated with $\beta_U$-invariant interactions
   376	$\Phi_{0},\Phi_{1}\in{\caJ}$.
   377	We say that  $H_0,H_1$ are
   378	$C^1$-equivalent with $\beta_U$-symmetry if the following conditions are satisfied.
   379	\begin{enumerate}
   380	\item There exists $m\in\nan$ and a continuous and piecewise $C^1$-path $\Phi:[0,1]\to {\caJ}_m$ such that $\Phi(0)=\Phi_0$, 
   381	$\Phi(1)= \Phi_1$.
   382	\item  Let   $H(t)$ be the Hamiltonian associated with $\Phi(t)$ for each $t\in[0,1]$.
   383	There are $\gamma>0$, $N_0\in\nan$, and finite intervals $I(t)=[a(t), b(t)]$, whose endpoints $a(t), b(t)$ smoothly depending on $t\in[0,1]$,
   384	such that for all finite intervals $\Lambda\subset\bbZ$ with $|\Lambda|\ge N_0$,
   385	the smallest eigenvalue of $H(t)_\Lambda$ is in $I(t)$ and
   386	the rest of the spectrum is in $[b(t)+\gamma,\infty)$.
   387	\item
   388	For each $t\in[0,1]$, $\Phi(t)$ is $\beta_U$-invariant.
   389	\end{enumerate}
   390	\end{defn}
   391	\begin{rem}
   392	We write $H_0\simeq_{C^1,U} H_1$ when
   393	$H_0,H_1$ are 
   394	$C^1$-equivalent with $\beta_U$-symmetry.
   395	If furthermore the path  $\Phi(t)$ can be taken in ${\caJ}_m$ we write
   396	$H_0\simeq_{C^1,U,m}H_1$.
   397	\end{rem}
   398	
   399	Now let us introduce the family of Hamiltonians we consider in this paper.
   400	As an input, we prepare the following triple.
   401	\begin{defn}\label{def:sp}
   402	Let $G$ be a group and 
   403	$c : G\times G\to \bbT$ a $2$-cocycle of $G$. Let $U:G\to \caU_n$ be a unitary representation of $G$ on $\bbC^n$.
   404	Let $k$ be a natural number, $u_g$ a projective unitary representation of $G$ on $\bbC^k$ with respect to
   405	the $2$-cocycle $c$,
   406	and $V$ an isometry from $\bbC^k$ to $\bbC^n\otimes\bbC^k$.
   407	We denote by $\caSP(n,G,U,c)$ the set of all such triple $(k, u, V)$ which satisfies the following conditions.
   408	\begin{enumerate}
   409	\item[(i)] For any $g\in G$, we have
   410	\begin{align}\label{eq:uui}
   411	\lmk {U_g}\otimes u_g\rmk V=V u_g.
   412	\end{align}
   413	\item[(ii)] Define $\vv=\vv_{(k,u,V)}=(v_1,\ldots, v_n)\in\mk^{\times n}$ by
   414	\begin{align}\label{eq:Vv}
   415	V x=\sum_{\mu=1}^{n}\psi_{\mu}\otimes v_{\mu}^* x,\quad
   416	x\in\bbC^k.
   417	\end{align}
   418	(Recall that $\{\psi_{\mu}\}_{\mu}$ is the fixed CONS of $\cc^n$.)
   419	Define the completely positive map $T_{\vv}\;:\; \mk\to\mk$ by
   420	\begin{align}
   421	T_{\vv}(X):=\sum_{\mu=1}^n v_{\mu}Xv_{\mu}^*,\quad X\in\mk.
   422	\end{align}
   423	Then $T_{\vv}$ is primitive.
   424	\end{enumerate}
   425	\end{defn}
   426	The definition of primitivity can be found in \cite{Wolf:2012aa}, for example.
   427	
   428	From the $n$-tuple of $k\times k$ matrices $\vv=\vv_{(k,u,V)}=(v_1,\ldots, v_n)\in\mk^{\times n}$
   429	associated with $(k, u, V)\in \caSP(n,G,U,c)$, we construct our interaction following the recipe of
   430	\cite{Fannes:1992vq}.
   431	For $l\in\nan$ and $\mu^{(l)}=(\mu_0,\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{l-1})\in\{1,\cdots,n\}^{\times l}$,
   432	we use the notation
   433	\begin{align}
   434	\widehat{v_{\mu^{(l)}}}:=
   435	v_{\mu_0}v_{\mu_1}\cdots v_{\mu_{l-1}}\in\mk,\quad
   436	\widehat{\psi_{\mu^{(l)}}}:=
   437	\bigotimes_{i=0}^{l-1}\psi_{\mu_i}\in\bigotimes_{i=0}^{l-1}\cc^n.
   438	\end{align}
   439	\begin{defn}
   440	Let  $(k, u, V)\in \caSP(n,G,U,c)$ and $\vv=\vv_{(k,u,V)}=(v_1,\ldots, v_n)\in\mk^{\times n}$ the $n$-tuple
   441	associated to it.
   442	For each $l\in\nan$,
   443	define
   444	$\Gamma_{l,\vv}\;:\; \mk\to\bigotimes_{i=0}^{l-1}\cc^n$
   445	by
   446	\begin{align}\label{eq:gbdef}
   447	\Gamma_{l,\vv}\lmk X\rmk
   448	=\sum_{\mu^{(l)}\in \{1,\cdots,n\}^{\times l}}\lmk\Tr X \lmk\widehat{v_{\mu^{(l)}}} \rmk^*\rmk\widehat{\psi_{\mu^{(l)}}},\quad
   449	X\in\mk,
   450	\end{align}
   451	and set
   452	$
   453	\cgv{l}:=\Ran\Gamma_{l,\vv}\subset \bigotimes_{i=0}^{l-1}\cc^n.
   454	$
   455	Furthermore, we denote by
   456	$G_{l,\vv}$
   457	the orthogonal projection onto $\cgv{l}$ in $\bigotimes_{i=0}^{l-1}\cc^n$.
   458	We set $h_{l,\vv}:=1-G_{l,\vv}$ and $\Phi_{l,\vv}:=\Phi_{h_{l,\vv}}$.
   459	(Recall (\ref{hamdef}).)
   460	\end{defn}
   461	
   462	It is known that this recipe gives a Hamiltonian with a spectral gap \cite{Fannes:1992vq}.
   463	\begin{lem}\label{lem:spgap}
   464	Let  $(k, u, V)\in \caSP(n,G,U,c)$ and $\vv=\vv_{(k,u,V)}=(v_1,\ldots, v_n)\in\mk^{\times n}$ the $n$-tuple
   465	associated to it.
   466	Then, for any $m\in\nan$, the interaction $\Phi_{m,\vv}$ is $\beta_U$-invariant.
   467	For $m\ge k^4+1$ the Hamiltonian $H_{\Phi_{m,\vv}}$ is gapped.
   468	\end{lem}
   469	\begin{proof}
   470	To show the first statement, let us consider a positive operator
   471	\[
   472	X_m:=\sum_{\mu^{(m)},\nu^{(m)}\in \{1,\cdots,n\}^{\times m}}
   473	\lmk \Tr \lmk \lmk\widehat{v_{\mu^{(m)}}} \rmk^*\widehat{v_{\nu^{(m)}}} \rmk
   474	\rmk
   475	\ket{\widehat{\psi_{\mu^{(m)}}}}\bra{\widehat{\psi_{\nu^{(m)}}}}.
   476	\]
   477	We claim that the support $s(X_m)$ of $X_m$ is $G_{m,\vv}$.
   478	To see $G_{m,\vv}\le s(X_m)$, let us consider an arbitrary $\xi\in \bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\cc^n$, and
   479	set $Z_{\xi}:=\sum_{\mu^{(m)}}\braket{\xi}{\widehat{\psi_{\mu^{(m)}}}}\lmk\widehat{v_{\mu^{(m)}}} \rmk^*\in\mk$.
   480	For any $Y\in\mk$, we have $\braket{\xi}{\Gamma_{m,\vv}(Y)}=\Tr Y Z_\xi$.
   481	We also have $\braket{\xi}{X\xi}=\Tr Z_{\xi} Z_{\xi}^*$.
   482	Therefore, from the Cauchy-Schwartz inequality, we obtain
   483	\[
   484	\lv \braket{\xi}{\Gamma_{m,\vv}(Y)}\rv^2=\lv \Tr Y Z_\xi\rv^2
   485	\le \Tr Y Y^*\cdot \Tr Z_\xi^*Z_\xi
   486	=\Tr Y Y^*\cdot \braket{\xi}{X\xi},
   487	\]
   488	for any  $Y\in\mk$ and $\xi\in \bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\cc^n$. This implies $G_{m,\vv}\le s(X_m)$.
   489	To prove $G_{m,\vv}\ge s(X_m)$,
   490	let $\{\ek{i,j}\}_{i,j=1}^k$ be the matrix units of $\mk$.
   491	By the straightforward  calculation, we obtain
   492	\[
   493	X_m=\sum_{i,j=1}^k \ket{\Gamma_{m,\vv}\lmk \ek{i,j}\rmk}\bra{\Gamma_{m,\vv}\lmk \ek{i,j}\rmk}.
   494	\]
   495	This proves $G_{m,\vv}\ge s(X_m)$.
   496	
   497	From this, we see that in order to show $G_{m,\vv}$ is $\beta_U$-invariant, it suffices to show that $X_m$ is $\beta_U$-invariant.
   498	For this, we 
   499	define a completely positive map $\bbE: \mn\otimes\mn\to\mk$ by
   500	\[
   501	\bbE\lmk A\otimes X\rmk := V^* \lmk A\otimes X\rmk V 
   502	=\sum_{\mu,\nu=1}^{n} \braket{\psi_\mu}{A\psi_{\nu}} v_{\mu} X v_{\nu}^*
   503	\quad
   504	A\in \mn,\quad X\in\mk.
   505	\]
   506	For each $A\in\mn$, we set $\bbE_A:\mk\to\mk$ by $\bbE_A(X)=V^* \lmk A\otimes X\rmk V$, 
   507	$X\in\mk$.
   508	From (i) of the Definition \ref{def:sp}, we have
   509	\begin{align}\label{eq:aug}
   510	&\bbE_{\Ad\lmk  {U_g}^*\rmk\lmk A\rmk}\lmk X\rmk
   511	= V^* \lmk {U_g}^*A {U_g}\otimes X\rmk V
   512	=V^*\lmk {U_g}\otimes u_g\rmk^* \lmk A\otimes u_gXu_g^* \rmk
   513	\lmk {U_g}\otimes u_g\rmk V\notag\\
   514	&=\lmk V u_g\rmk^* \lmk A\otimes u_gXu_g^* \rmk
   515	V u_g
   516	=\Ad u_g^*\circ \bbE_{A}\circ \Ad u_g\lmk X\rmk.
   517	\end{align}
   518	(Here $\Ad \lmk {U_g}^*\rmk(X):={U_g}^* X {U_g}$ etc.)
   519	On the other hand, by the straightforward calculation we obtain the following formula for any $A_0,\ldots, A_{m-1}\in\mn$,
   520	\begin{align}\label{eq:xfm}
   521	\Tr_{\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\mn}\lmk X_m
   522	\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1} A_i\rmk
   523	=\Tr_{\mk}\lmk \bbE_{A_0}\circ\cdots\circ\bbE_{A_{m-1}}\lmk 1\rmk\rmk.
   524	\end{align}
   525	Now we show the $\beta_U$-invariance of $X_m$.
   526	For all $A_0,\ldots, A_{m-1}\in\mn$,
   527	\begin{align*}
   528	&\Tr_{\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\mn}\lmk \Ad\lmk  U_g^{\bigotimes m-1}\rmk \lmk X_m\rmk
   529	\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1} A_i\rmk
   530	=\Tr_{\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\mn}\lmk  X_m
   531	\lmk \bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1} \Ad \lmk U_g^*\rmk\lmk A_i\rmk\rmk \rmk\\
   532	&=\Tr_{\mk}\lmk \bbE_{\Ad U_g^*\lmk A_0\rmk}\circ\cdots\circ\bbE_{\Ad U_g^*\lmk A_{m-1}\rmk}\lmk 1\rmk\rmk
   533	=\Tr_{\mk}\lmk \bbE_{A_0}\circ\cdots\circ\bbE_{A_{m-1}}\lmk 1\rmk\rmk
   534	=\Tr_{\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1}\mn}\lmk X_m
   535	\bigotimes_{i=0}^{m-1} A_i\rmk.
   536	\end{align*}
   537	Here, in the second and the fourth equality, we used (\ref{eq:xfm}), and for the third equality, we used 
   538	(\ref{eq:aug}).
   539	This proves $\beta_{U_g}(X_m)=X_m$, for all $g\in G$.
   540	
   541	The second statement follows from \cite{Fannes:1992vq}, except for the
   542	condition $m\ge k^4+1$ which is discussed in Lemma 3.1 of \cite{bo}
   543	and basically quantum {Wielandt's} inequality \cite{Sanz:2010aa}.
   544	\end{proof}
   545	
   546	\begin{defn}
   547	Let $G$ be a group and 
   548	$c : G\times G\to \bbT$ a $2$-cocycle of $G$.
   549	Let $(u_0, \bbC^{k_0})$, $(u_1,\bbC^{k_1})$
   550	be finite dimensional projective unitary representations of  $G$ with respect to the $2$-cocycle $c$.
   551	We say $(u^{(0)}, \bbC^{k_0})$ and $(u^{(1)},\bbC^{k_1})$ are unitary equivalent if $k:=k_0=k_1$ and
   552	there exists a unitary matrix $W\in\caU_k$ such that 
   553	$W u^{(0)}_g=u^{(1)}_gW$, for all $g\in G$.
   554	\end{defn}
   555	Here is the main Theorem of this paper.
   556	\begin{thm}\label{thm:gmain}
   557	Let $G$ be a group and 
   558	$c : G\times G\to \bbT$ a $2$-cocycle of $G$. Let $U:G\to \caU_n$ be a unitary representation of $G$ on $\bbC^n$.
   559	Let $(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$ and $(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$ be elements in 
   560	$\caSP(n,G,U,c)$, and $\vv_0:=\vv(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$,
   561	 $\vv_1:=\vv(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$, the $n$-tuples associated to them via (\ref{eq:Vv}).
   562	Assume that the projective representations $(u^{(0)}, \bbC^{k_0})$ and $(u^{(1)},\bbC^{k_1})$ are unitary equivalent.
   563	Then we have $H_{\Phi_{m,\vv_0}}\simeq_{C^1,U,m}
   564	H_{\Phi_{m,\vv_1}}$, for any $m\ge k^4+1$.
   565	\end{thm}
   566	\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:gmain}}
   567	We introduce an equivalence relation in $\caSP(n,G,U,c)$.
   568	\begin{defn}\label{def:spath}
   569	Let $(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$ and $(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$ be elements in 
   570	$\caSP(n,G,U,c)$. We say $(k^{(0)}, u^{(0)}, V^{(0)})$ and $(k^{(1)}, u^{(1)}, V^{(1)})$ are equivalent,
   571	 if the following holds.
   572	\begin{enumerate}
   573	\item$k:=k^{(0)}=k^{(1)}$.
   574	\item There exists a map $u:[0,1]\times G\to \caU_{k}$ such that
   575	for any $g\in G$, $[0,1]\ni t\to u(\cdot,g)\in \caU_{k}$ is $C^{\infty}$.

