503:\section{The split property}
504-\label{s:split}
505:Before going into the proofs, we review an important notion known as the split property in operator algebra.
506-We in particular recall two results which are  essential for us.
507-
508:We here give the following definition of the split property, which is most suitable for our purpose.
509-It corresponds to the standard definition \cite{dl} in our setting (see \cite{Matsui2}).
510-See Definition~1.1 of \cite{Matsui2} for a different characterization, which is more physically motivated.
511-\begin{defn}
512:Let $\varphi$ be a pure state on $\caA$. Let $\varphi_R$ be the ristriciton of
513-$\varphi$ to $\caA_R$, and $(\caH,\pi,\Omega)$ be the GNS triple of $\varphi_R$.
514:We say $\varphi$ satisfies the split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$,
515-if the von Neumann algebra $\pi(\caA_{R})''$ is a type I factor.
516-\end{defn}
517:The important connection between this abstract notion and our physical problem is given by the following result due to Matsui, which is based on Hastings's area law theorem \cite{area}.
518-\begin{thm}[Corollary 3.2 of \cite{Matsui2}]\label{matsui}
519:Let $\varphi$ be a (not necessarily unique) pure $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state of a translation invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$, and denote by  $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ the corresponding bulk Hamiltonian. 
520-Assume that $0$ is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ and
521-there exists $\gamma>0$ such that $\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})\setminus\{0\}\subset [\gamma,\infty)$.
522:Then $\varphi$ satisfies the  split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$.
523-\end{thm}
524-Recall that a type I factor is isomorphic to $B(\caK)$, the set of all bounded operators 
525-on a  Hilbert space $\caK$. 
526-See \cite{takesaki}.
527-It has been shown in \cite{arv}, on the other hand, that a unital endomorphism of
528-$B(\caK)$ gives a representation of the Cuntz algebra.
529:Since the translation to the right by unit distance in the spin chain induces an endomorphism on $\pi(\caA_{R})''$ when $\varphi$ is translation invariant,
530-the following MPS-like object shows up.
531-
532-\begin{thm}[\cite{arv,bjp,BJ}, Proof of Proposition 3.5 of \cite{Matsui1} and Lemma 3.5 of \cite{Matsui3}]\label{cun}
533:Let $\varphi$ be a translation invariant pure split state on $\caA$.
534-Let $\varphi_R$ be the restriction of $\varphi$ to $\caA_R$, and $(\caH,\pi,\Omega)$ be the GNS triple of $\varphi_R$.
535-Then there exist operators $\cs_\mu\in \pi(\caA_R)''$ with $\mu\in\SSS$
536-satisfying the following:
537-\begin{align}
538-&\cs_{\mu}^*\cs_{\nu}=\delta_{\mu\nu}\unit,\label{s1}\\
539-&\sum_{\mu\in\SSS} \cs_{\mu} \pi_{}(A) \cs_{\mu}^*=\pi\circ\tau_{1}(A),\quad A\in \caA_R \label{st}.\\
540-&\pi\lmk e_{\mu\nu}\otimes\unit_{[1,\infty)}\rmk
541-=\cs_\mu \cs_\nu^*\quad
--
544-Here $ e_{\mu\nu}\otimes\unit_{[1,\infty)}$ indicates element $e_{\mu\nu}$ in $\caA_{\{0\}}=\Mat_{2S+1}$
545-embedded into $\caA_R$.
546-\end{thm}
547-This theorem enables us to extend the argument for MPS to our general setting.
