   761	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   762	\section{Setting and main results}
   763	\label{s:SR}
   764	\paragraph*{C$^*$-algebras and split states}
   765	We start by defining a general quantum spin system on the infinite chain $\bbZ$.
   766	For each site\footnote{%
   767	A site may be a collection of sites in the standard sense.
   768	} $x\in\bbZ$ we associate a Hilbert space $\hilb_x\cong\bbC^{d_x}$ with dimension $d_x\in\bbN$. 
   769	For a finite subset $\La\in\bbZ$, we define the algebra $\OA_\La$ of local observables as the set of all bounded operators on the Hilbert space $\bigotimes_{x\in\La}\hilb_x$.
   770	For finite subsets $\La\subset\La'\subset\bbZ$, the algebra $\OA_\La$ is naturally embedded in $\OA_{\La'}$ by tensoring its elements by identity.
   771	For any infinite subset $\Gamma\subset\bbZ$, we denote by $\OA_\Gamma$ the inductive limit of the collection of algebras $\OA_\La$ with $\La$ being an arbitrary finite subset of $\Gamma$.
   772	The C$^*$-algebra of the whole chain is then denoted as $\OA=\OA_\bbZ$.
   773	We also introduce the C$^*$-algebras for half-infinite chains by $\AL_x=\OA_{\{\ldots,x-1,x\}}$ and $\AR_x=\OA_{\{x,x+1,\ldots\}}$, where $x\in\bbZ$.
   774	Note that $\AL_x$ and $\AR_x$ can naturally be regarded as subalgebras of $\OA$.
   775	
   776	We define states on the C$^*$-algebras as usual.
   777	The notion of split states is essential.
   778	\begin{D}
   779	\label{D:split}
   780	Let $\rho$ be a pure state on $\OA$, and denote by $\rho^\rL_0$ and $\rho^\rR_1$ be the restrictions of $\rho$ onto the subalgebras $\AL_0$ and $\AR_1$, respectively.
   781	We say that $\rho$ satisfies the split property if $\rho$ and  $\rho^\rL_0\otimes\rho^\rR_1$ are quasi-equivalent.
   782	\end{D}
   783	It is easily seen that one may replace  $\rho^\rL_0$ and $\rho^\rR_1$ in the above definition by  $\rho^\rL_x$ and $\rho^\rR_{x+1}$ with an arbitrary $x\in\bbZ$, which are the restrictions of $\rho$ onto $\AL_x$ and $\AR_{x+1}$, respectively.
   784	
   785	In the main theorems, Theorems~\ref{T:trans} and \ref{T:ref}, we state necessary conditions for the existence of a pure split state.
   786	They can be rephrased as no-go theorems for unique gapped ground states since a unique gapped ground state (of a model with short range interactions) is known to satisfy the split property\cite{Matsui2}.\footnote{%
   787	It is also known that a state with area law entanglement satisfies the split property.
   788	Thus our theorems may be interpreted as no-go theorems for area law states.
   789	}
   790	See the end of section~\ref{s:proof1}. 
   791	
   792	\paragraph*{On-site symmetry}
   793	We always consider a model with certain on-site symmetry.
   794	Let $G$ be a finite group and fix a homomorphism $\ua:G\to\{1,-1\}$.
   795	For each $x\in\bbZ$, we assume that there is an operator $v_x(g)$ on $\hilb_x$ which is unitary if $\ua(g)=1$ and antiunitary if $\ua(g)=-1$, and that $v_x(\cdot)$ gives a projective representation of the group $G$.
   796	We denote by $c_x\in\SC$ the degree-2 cohomology class of the projective representation,
   797	as explained in section \ref{s:outlie}.
   798	
   799	We define the adjoint representation of $G$ by $\Xi(g)(A)=v_x(g)A(v_x(g))^*$ for $A\in\OA_{\{x\}}$.
   800	One can uniquely extend $\Xi(g)$ to  \mbox{$*$-automorphisms} on $\OA$, $\AL_x$, and $\AR_x$.
   801	The \mbox{$*$-automorphism} $\Xi(g)$ is linear if $\ua(g)=1$ and antilinear if $\ua(g)=-1$.
   802	Note that $\Xi(\cdot)$ gives a genuine representation of $G$, i.e., $\Xi(g)\circ\Xi(h)=\Xi(gh)$ for any $g,h\in G$.
   803	We say that a state $\rho$ on $\OA$ is $G$-invariant if $\rho(\Xi(g)(A))=\rho(A)$ when $\ua(g)=1$ and
   804	$\rho(\Xi(g)(A^{*}))=\rho(A)$ when $\ua(g)=-1$ for any $A\in\OA$.
   805	
   806	
   807	\paragraph{Examples in section~\ref{s:intro} in this language} 
   808	To see two examples discussed in section~\ref{s:intro}, we consider standard quantum spin systems on $\bbZ$.
   809	The dimension of the local Hilbert space $\hilb_x$ is given by $d_x=2\bar{S}_x+1$, where $\bar{S}_x\in\bbN/2$ is the spin quantum number at site $x\in\bbZ$.
   810	For a finite subset $\La\subset\bbZ$, the algebra $\OA_\La$ consists of polynomials of spin operators $S^{(\nu)}_x$ with $\nu=1,2,3$ and $x\in\La$;
   811	this is because  the operators $S^{(1,2,3)}_x$ generate the algebra $\OA_{\{x\}}$.
   812	
   813	To formulate $\ZZ$ transformation, we set\footnote{%
   814	The multiplication rule is $a_1a_2=a_3$, $a_2a_3=a_1$, $a_3a_1=a_2$, and $(a_\nu)^2=e$ for $\nu=1,2,3$.
   815	} $G=\ZZ=\{e,a_1,a_2,a_3\}$, and $\ua(g)=1$ for all $g\in G$.
   816	Then the second cohomology group is $H^2(\ZZ,\Uo)=\{0,1\}$.
   817	We define the projective representation on the local Hilbert space $\hilb_x$ by $v_x(e)=\Id$ and $v_x(a_\nu)=\exp[-i\pi S^{(\nu)}_x]$ for $\nu=1,2,3$.
   818	The degree-2 cohomology class of the projective representation is $c_x=0$ if $\bar{S}_x$ is an integer, and $c_x=1$ if $\bar{S}_x$ is a half-odd-integer.
   819	It is found that the corresponding adjoint representation satisfies
   820	\eq
   821	\Xi(a_\nu)(S^{(\mu)}_x)=\begin{cases}
   822	S^{(\mu)}_x&\nu=\mu,\\
   823	-S^{(\mu)}_x&\nu\ne\mu,
   824	\end{cases}
   825	\lb{ZZ}
   826	\en
   827	for any $x\in\bbZ$ and $\nu,\mu=1,2,3$.
   828	
   829	
   830	To formulate time-reversal transformation, we set $G=\bbZ_2=\{e,a\}$ and $\ua(e)=1$, $\ua(a)=-1$.
   831	Then one has $H^2(\bbZ_2,\Uu)=\{0,1\}$.
   832	We define the projective representation on $\hilb_x$ by $v_x(e)=\Id$ and $v_x(a)=K\,\exp[-i\pi S^{(2)}_x]$, where $K$ is the complex conjugation map.\footnote{%
   833	We here use the standard matrix representation of spin operators in which all the matrix entries of $S^{(2)}_x$ are pure imaginary.
   834	See, e.g., \cite[section~2.3]{TasakiBook} for details.
   835	}
   836	The degree-2 cohomology class of the projective representation is again $c_x=0$ if $\bar{S}_x$ is an integer, and $c_x=1$ if $\bar{S}_x$ is a half-odd-integer.
   837	The corresponding adjoint representation gives $\Xi(a)(S^{(\nu)}_x)=-S^{(\nu)}_x$ for any $x\in\bbZ$ and $\nu=1,2,3$.
   838	
   839	
   840	
   841	
   842	 
   843	
   844	\paragraph*{Translation symmetry}
   845	We shall describe a class of models with translation symmetry.
   846	Take $d_x=d$ for all $x\in\bbZ$.
   847	We can then regard each $\hilb_x$ as a copy of a single Hilbert space $\hilb_0\cong\bbC^d$.
   848	For any $x,y\in\bbZ$ and $A\in\OA_{\{x\}}$, we denote by $\tau_y(A)\in\OA_{\{x+y\}}$ the identical copy of $A$ in $\OA_{x+y}$.
   849	We also assume that the on-site symmetry transformation is chosen so that $v_{x+y}(g)$ is an identical copy of $v_x(g)$ on $\hilb_{x+y}$.
   850	We then have $c_x=c\in\SC$ for all $x\in\bbZ$.
   851	
   852	The translation $\tau_y$ automatically extends to a liner \mbox{$*$-automorphism} on $\OA$.
   853	We say that a state $\rho$ on $\OA$ is translation invariant if $\rho(\tau_y(A))=\rho(A)$ for any $y\in\bbZ$ and $A\in\OA$.
   854	Here we  prove the following theorem,
   855	which contains two theorems proved in \cite{OT1} and summarized as Corollary~\ref{C:trans} as special cases.
   856	\begin{T}\label{T:trans}
   857	Consider a system with translation symmetry, and let $\rho$ be a pure split state that is $G$-invariant and translation invariant.
   858	Then one inevitably has $c=0$.
   859	\end{T}
   860	
   861	
   862	
   863	\paragraph*{Reflection symmetry}
   864	We consider another class of models that are invariant under reflection about the origin of the chain (but not necessarily invariant under translation).
   865	Assume that the local dimensions satisfy $d_x=d_{-x}$ for all $x\in\bbZ$.
   866	We can then take the local Hilbert spaces $\hilb_{x}$ and $\hilb_{-x}$ to be identical.
   867	We assume that, for each $x\in\bbZ$, there is a linear \mbox{$*$-automorphism} $\calR_x:\OA_{\{x\}}\to\OA_{\{-x\}}$ such that $\calR_{-x}\circ\calR_{x}=\Id$.
   868	We also assume that $v_{-x}(g)$ is an identical copy on $\hilb_{-x}$ of $v_x(g)$.
   869	
   870	
   871	From $\calR_x$ with all $x\in\bbZ$, one can define a linear \mbox{$*$-automorphism} $\calR$ on $\OA$ such that $\calR(A)=\calR_x(A)$ for $A\in \OA_{\{x\}}$.
   872	We say that a state $\rho$ on $\OA$ is reflection invariant if $\rho(\calR(A))=\rho(A)$ for any  $A\in\OA$.
   873	Then the following is a general form of our new Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorem.
   874	\begin{T}\label{T:ref}
   875	Consider a system with reflection symmetry, and let $\rho$ be a pure split state that is $G$-invariant and reflection invariant.
   876	Then one inevitably has $c_0=2c$ with some $c\in\SC$.
   877	\end{T}
   878	As we have already noted, the conclusion of the theorem implies $c_0=0$ when $\SC=\{0,1\}$ as in
   879	the two models with $\ZZ$ or time-reversal symmetry discussed in section~\ref{s:intro}.
   880	We also note that, by using the original idea of Lieb, Schultz, and Mattis, one can prove a similar (but different) theorem for a class of quantum spin chains with $\Uo\rtimes\bbZ_2$ symmetry.
   881	See Appendix~\ref{app-U(1)}.
   882	
   883	\begin{figure}
   884	\centering
   885	\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{foo}
   886	\caption{%
   887	The lattice $\La_m$ consists of the central site (black dot) and $m$ semi-infinite chains attached to it. The figure is for $m=5$.
   888	\label{fig:foo}}
   889	\end{figure}
   890	
   891	There is a generalization of Theorem~\ref{T:ref} to $\bbZ_m$ invariant quantum spin system on the lattice $\Lambda_m\coloneqq\{o\}\cup\{(i,x)\mid i=1,\ldots,m, x=1,2,\ldots\}$, which consists of the central site $o$ and $m$ semi-infinite chains attached to it.
   892	See Fig.~\ref{fig:foo} for the case $m=5$. 
   893	We associate with the central site $o$ a Hilbert space $\hilb_o$ and a projective representation $v_o(\cdot)$ of $G$.
   894	We impose $\mathbb{Z}_m$ symmetry by requiring that, for each $x=1,2,\ldots$, the Hilbert space $\hilb_{(i,x)}$ associated with site $(i,x)$ for $i=1,\ldots,m$ is an identical copy of a single Hilbert space $\hilb_x$, and also that the corresponding projective representation $v_{(i,x)}(\cdot)$ is identical to  $v_x(\cdot)$.
   895	We consider the transformation that shifts the chain-index as $i\to i+1$, where we identify $m+1$ with 1.
   896	This defines $\mathbb{Z}_m$ symmetry.
   897	
   898	To define the split property for this system,
   899	we note that the quantum spin system on $\Lambda_m$ can be regarded as a quantum spin chain by identifying the central site $o\in\Lambda_m$ with the origin $0\in\bbZ$, the site $(1,x)\in\Lambda_m$ with $x\in\bbZ$, and the collection of $m-1$ sites $(2,x),\ldots,(m,x)$ with $-x\in\bbZ$.
   900	We then say that a $\bbZ_m$-invariant state $\rho$ on $\Lambda_m$ satisfies the split property if the  state on $\bbZ$ obtained by the above identification satisfies the split property.
   901	
   902	\begin{T}\label{T:Zm}
   903	Consider the quantum spin system on $\La_m$, and let $\rho$ be a $\bbZ_m$ and $G$-invariant pure state that satisfies the split property.
   904	Then one inevitably has $c_o=mc$ with some $c\in\SC$.
   905	\end{T}
   906	Clearly Theorem \ref{T:ref} is a special case of Theorem \ref{T:Zm} for $m=2$.
   907	
