  1300	\appendix
  1301	\section{Generalization to compact Lie groups}
  1302	\label{app:Lie}
  1303	
  1304	In the main part of the paper we assumed that the on-site symmetry group $G$ is finite.
  1305	Here we outline how to generalize our setup to cover the case where the on-site symmetry group $G$ is a compact Lie group.
  1306	This generalization was first considered in \cite{DuivenvoordenQuella1}.
  1307	
  1308	We note that the original theorem of Lieb, Schultz, Mattis given in \cite[Appendix B]{LSM} was for the $SU(2)$-symmetric Heisenberg chain, which can be thought of as an example of our general theorem when $G=SO(3)$, or $G=\ZZ\subset SO(3)$, as we used in section~\ref{s:intro}.
  1309	Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorems for compact Lie groups for more general compact groups have also been discussed in the literature, see \cite{YHO} and the references therein.
  1310	
  1311	The main technical issue is that the definition of the group cohomology associated to a projective representation, given in section~\ref{s:outlie}, requires modifications when $G$ is a compact Lie group.
  1312	Just as we want a representation $V(g)$ of a Lie group $G$ to be a continuous map,
  1313	we need to impose some appropriate conditions on the cocycle $\varphi(g,h)$ as a function on $G\times G$.
  1314	
  1315	We split the discussions in two cases, namely i) when the compact group $G$ is connected and the corresponding Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ is semisimple, and then ii) when $G$ is a more general compact Lie group.
  1316	We give a proof in the case i); we only give an indication of a proof in the case ii).
  1317	The essential idea in the case i) is to find a suitable choice of finite subgroups $G_i$ of $G$  so that the projective representations of $G$ can be captured by those of $G_i$. 
  1318	This approach was already studied e.g.~in \cite{ElseBartlettDoherty,DuivenvoordenQuella2} when $G$ is a classical simple group except $Spin(4n)$.
  1319	Here we give a general construction applicable for arbitrary connected semisimple groups, based on a mathematical result \cite{Borel:1999bx}.
  1320	The main point in the case ii) is that a cohomology theory suitable for characterizing projective representations of continuous groups was already given by Mackey and Moore \cite{Mackey,Moore1,Moore2}.
  1321	
  1322	\paragraph{When $G$ is a compact connected semisimple group}
  1323	In this case, let $\tilde G$ be the universal covering group of $G$ such that $G=\tilde G/H$ where $H$ is an abelian normal subgroup of $\tilde G$ so that we have the extension \begin{equation}
  1324	0\longrightarrow H\longrightarrow \tilde G \stackrel{p}{\longrightarrow} G \longrightarrow 0.
  1325	\end{equation}
  1326	We note that $H=\pi_1(G)$.
  1327	
  1328	Let us consider a translation-invariant quantum spin chain where the Hilbert space $\hilb_x$ at each site is in a representation of $\tilde G$.
  1329	$\OA_x$ carries an adjoint action of $\tilde G$. 
  1330	Suppose that $\hilb_x$ as a representation of $H$ is a direct sum of a single irreducible representation $c$ of $H$.
  1331	Then $H$ acts trivially on $\OA_x$. 
  1332	Therefore $\OA_x$  is a representation of $G$, and therefore $\OA$ is a representation of $G$.
  1333	The version of theorem \ref{T:trans} in this setting is the following:
  1334	\begingroup
  1335	\renewcommand\thetheorem{\ref{T:trans}'}
  1336	\begin{T}
  1337	Consider a system with translation symmetry.
  1338	Suppose there is a pure split state $\rho$ that is $G$-invariant and translation invariant.
  1339	Then $c$ is the trivial representation of $H=\pi_1(G)$.
  1340	\end{T}
  1341	\endgroup
  1342	The proof of the theorem \ref{T:ref} for compact connected semisimple $G$ is entirely similar, so we omit it.
  1343	Our proof relies on the following lemma \ref{Le:trick}.
  1344	
  1345	To motivate the context of the lemma, consider the case $G=SO(3)$, for which we have $\tilde G=SU(2)$ and $H=\mathbb{Z}_2$.
  1346	Recall that we used $G'=\ZZ\subset SO(3)$ as one of the main examples in the main part of the paper.
  1347	Let $\tilde G':=p^{-1}(G')$ so that $G'=\tilde G'/H$.
  1348	We then have the commutative diagram of extensions \begin{equation}
  1349	\begin{array}{ccccccc}
  1350	0 \longrightarrow& H&\longrightarrow& \tilde G& \longrightarrow&  G & \longrightarrow 0  \\
  1351	& \rotatebox{90}{$=$} & & \cup &&\cup \\
  1352	0 \longrightarrow& H&\longrightarrow& \tilde G'& \longrightarrow&  G' & \longrightarrow 0  
  1353	\end{array}.
  1354	\label{eq:com}
  1355	\end{equation}
  1356	An element $c\in \hat H$ then gives an extension of $G'$ by $\Uo$, therefore we have a homomorphism \begin{equation}
  1357	\iota: \hat H \to  H^2(G',\Uo).\label{eq:iota}
  1358	\end{equation}
  1359	For $G'=\ZZ$ this homomorphism $\iota$ is an isomorphism;
  1360	equivalently, $\tilde G'$ is the representation group of $G'=\ZZ$ in the sense of Schur,
  1361	i.e.~$\tilde G'$ is an extension of $G'$ by $H$ such that 
  1362	any projective representation of $G'$ is a genuine representation of $\tilde G'$ and $\hat H=H^2(G',U(1))$.
  1363	This allows us to reduce the LSM theorem for $G=SO(3)$ to the LSM theorem for $G'=\ZZ$.
  1364	
  1365	This construction directly generalize when $\tilde G=SU(n)$, $H=\mathbb{Z}_n$ and $G=PSU(n)$.
  1366	In this case we consider two elements $a,b\in \tilde G$ given by \begin{align}
  1367	\label{standardab}
  1368	a&=\gamma\begin{pmatrix}
  1369	1 \\
  1370	& c  \\
  1371	&&c ^2 \\
  1372	&&&\ddots \\
  1373	&&&&c ^{n-2}\\
  1374	&&&&&c ^{n-1}\\
  1375	\end{pmatrix},&
  1376	b&=\gamma\begin{pmatrix}
  1377	0 & 1\\
  1378	&0&1\\
  1379	&&0&1\\
  1380	&&&\ddots &\ddots \\
  1381	&&&&0&1\\
  1382	1&&&&&0
  1383	\end{pmatrix},
  1384	\end{align}
  1385	where $c $ is a primitive $n$-th roots of unity and $\gamma^n=c ^{n(n-1)/2}$.
  1386	They satisfy $ab=c  ba$. 
  1387	Furthermore,  we have \begin{equation}
  1388	\label{abo}
  1389	a^n=b^n=\begin{cases}
  1390	1 & (n: \text{odd}),\\
  1391	c ^{n/2} & (n:\text{even}).
  1392	\end{cases}
  1393	\end{equation}
  1394	Then $\tilde G'=\langle a,b\rangle$ and $p(\tilde G')=G'=\mathbb{Z}_n\times\mathbb{Z}_n$  fit in the commutative diagram \eqref{eq:com} above.
  1395	We can now reduce the LSM theorem for $G=PSU(n)$ to that for $G'=\mathbb{Z}_n\times \mathbb{Z}_n$.
  1396	
  1397	
  1398	In general, two elements $a,b\in \tilde G$ in a  connected simply-connected Lie group such that $ab=c  ba$ where $c \in H$ are said to form an almost commuting pair, and all such pairs for an arbitrary $c$ were described in \cite{Schweigert:1996tg,Borel:1999bx,Kac:1999gw} for arbitrary Dynkin type. 
  1399	We will make use of the following lemma concerning almost commuting pairs:
  1400	\begin{Le}[\protect{=\cite[Corollary 4.2.1]{Borel:1999bx}}\ ]
  1401	\label{Le:abo}
  1402	Let $\tilde G$ be simply-connected semisimple compact group and $H$ be its center.
  1403	For any $c\in H$ of order $n$, there is an almost commuting pair $a,b\in G$ such that $ab=c ba$ satisfying \eqref{abo}. 
  1404	\end{Le}
  1405	
  1406	From this lemma we derive another lemma given below:
  1407	
  1408	\begin{Le}\label{Le:trick}
  1409	For any compact connected semisimple group $G$, there is a finite collection of finite subgroups $G'_i$ which fits in the commutative diagram
  1410	\begin{equation}
  1411	\begin{array}{ccccccc}
  1412	0 \longrightarrow& H&\longrightarrow& \tilde G& \longrightarrow&  G & \longrightarrow 0  \\
  1413	& \cup & & \cup &&\cup \\
  1414	0 \longrightarrow& H_i&\longrightarrow& \tilde G'_i& \longrightarrow&  G'_i & \longrightarrow 0  
  1415	\end{array}
  1416	\end{equation}
  1417	such that we have $\hat H_i \simeq H^2(G'_i,\Uo)$,
  1418	 $\prod H_i \simeq H$,
  1419	%\item 
  1420	and therefore
  1421	%\item 
  1422	$\hat H\simeq \prod \hat H_i \simeq \bigoplus  H^2(G'_i,\Uo)$.
  1423	%\end{itemize}
  1424	\end{Le}
  1425	
  1426	We provide the proof of the theorem first, and then that of the lemma.
  1427	
  1428	\paragraph{Proof of the theorem \ref{T:trans}'}
  1429	A $G$-symmetric system is also $G'_i$-symmetric for each $i$.
  1430	We assumed that $\hilb_x$ is a direct sum of a single irreducible representation $c\in \hat H$.
  1431	This means that as a $G'_i$-symmetric system, the degree-2 cohomology class associated to $\hilb_x$ is given by $c_i\in H^2(G'_i,\Uo)$, where $c_i$ is the $i$-th direct sum component of $c$ in the decomposition $\hat H\simeq \bigoplus H^2(G'_i,\Uo)$.
  1432	We use the original Theorem~\ref{T:trans} and conclude that $c_i=0$ for each $i$.
  1433	We therefore conclude $c=0$.
  1434	\qedm
  1435	
  1436	\paragraph{Proof of the lemma \ref{Le:trick}}
  1437	$H$ is a finite Abelian group, and therefore a product of cyclic groups, $H=\prod_i \mathbb{Z}_{k_i}$.
  1438	Let $\omega_i$ be the generator  of $\mathbb{Z}_{k_i}$.
  1439	From Lemma~\ref{Le:abo}, there is an almost commuting pair $a_i,b_i\in \tilde G$ for $\omega_i$.
  1440	We take $\tilde G'_i=\langle a,b\rangle$ and $G'_i=p(\tilde G'_i)$.
  1441	We have $G'_i=\mathbb{Z}_{k_i}\times \mathbb{Z}_{k_i}$ and $\tilde G'_i$ is its extension by $\mathbb{Z}_{k_i}$.
  1442	The resulting group is well-known to be  the representation group of $G'_i$.
  1443	\qedm
  1444	\if0
  1445	\medskip
  1446	
  1447	As it is somewhat difficult to extract the lemma~\ref{Le:abo} from \cite{Borel:1999bx}, we give an outline of its proof:
  1448	\paragraph{Proof of the lemma \ref{Le:abo}}
  1449	We first fix a maximal torus $T\in \tilde G$ containing $c $. We then write \begin{equation}
  1450	c =\exp(\sum_{a\in \Delta} \lambda_a a^\vee)
  1451	\end{equation} where $\Delta$ is the set of simple roots and $a^\vee$ is the coroot for the simple root $a\in \Delta$.
  1452	$\lambda_a$ is well-defined  modulo $\mathbb{Z}$.
  1453	We let $\mathfrak{t}$ be the Lie algebra of $T$ and we let \begin{equation}
  1454	\mathfrak{t}_c = \bigcup_{{a\in \Delta,  \lambda_a \not\in \mathbb{Z}}} \mathrm{Ker}\ a.
  1455	\end{equation}
  1456	We then let $S_c $ be the subtorus of $T$ whose Lie algebra is $\mathfrak{t}_c $,
  1457	and then let $L_c $ be the derived subgroup of the centralizer of $S_c $.
  1458	From \cite[Corollary 3.4.2]{Borel:1999bx},
  1459	we have 
  1460	$c \in L_c $,
  1461	$L_c  \simeq \prod_i SU(n_i) $ for some integers $n_i\ge 2$,
  1462	and $c =\prod_i c_i$ where $c_i$ generates the center of $SU(n_i)$.
  1463	We finally let $a=\prod_i a_{n_i}$ and $b=\prod_i b_{n_i}$ where $a_n$ and $b_n$ were given in \eqref{standardab}, and we are done. \qedm
  1464	\fi
  1465	
  1466	\paragraph{When $G$ is a general compact Lie group}
  1467	The discussion above is not  satisfactory, if one wants to consider the cases when $G$ is not necessarily connected, e.g.~$G=O(n)$.
  1468	In such cases it is not clear to the authors whether we can always choose finite subgroups $G'_i$ of $G$ so that the LSM theorem for $G$ can be deduced from the LSM theorems for $G'_i$ as we did above.
  1469	
  1470	Instead, we can use a more general theory of projective representations of locally compact Lie groups $G$ developed in \cite{Mackey,Moore1,Moore2}.
  1471	There, the group cohomology $H^2(G,\Uu)$ is defined by placing the condition that the cochains $\varphi(g,h)$ on $G\times G$ is a Borel function.
  1472	When $G$ is connected and semisimple, the group cohomology defined in this manner is known to agree with $\widehat{\pi_1(G)}$ as used above \cite[Proposition 2.1]{Moore1}.
  1473	
  1474	Another important theorem \cite[Theorem 2.2]{Mackey} for our purpose states that any continuous homomorphism $G\to PU(\mathcal{H})$
  1475	determines a unique class in $H^2(G,U(1))$, 
  1476	where $PU(\mathcal{H})=U(\mathcal{H})/U(1)$ is the projective unitary group for a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$.
  1477	Using this theorem instead of Wigner's theorem, 
  1478	we should be able to  attach the degree-2 classes $\sigma^{\rL}_x,\sigma^{\rR}_x \in H^2(G,\Uu)$ as in the finite group case, see the discussions around \eqref{e:VXV}.
  1479	The proof of the crucial lemma \ref{L:main} then goes mostly unchanged.
  1480	
  1481	\paragraph{Comment on the case $G=U(1)$}
  1482	Our approach does not say anything nontrivial when $G=U(1)$, since $H^2(U(1),\Uo)=\{0\}$ when defined using the Borel functions.
  1483	This is unfortunate, since early extensions of the original theorem of LSM were mainly about relaxing the $SO(3)$ symmetry to its $U(1)$ subgroup. 
  1484	One important result in this direction was obtained in \cite{OYA}, where it was shown that there is no unique gapped ground state if the filling factor $\nu$ of a $U(1)$-symmetric system is not an integer. 
  1485	
  1486	More recently, in \cite{CHR}, the various LSM theorems were given an interpretation in terms of the mixed anomaly between the on-site symmetry $G$ and the lattice translation symmetry $\mathbb{Z}$.
  1487	This is captured by an element in \begin{equation}
  1488	H^3(B(G\times \mathbb{Z}),\Uo)  / H^3(BG,\Uo) =H^2(BG,\Uo)
  1489	\end{equation} where $BG$ is the classifying space of $G$.
  1490	In many cases the group cohomology $H^2(BG,\Uo)$ defined in terms of the classifying space 
  1491	reduces to $H^2(G,\Uo)$ defined using cocycles on the group manifold. 
  1492	When $G=U(1)$, however, this is not so, and $H^2(BU(1),\Uo)=U(1)=\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$.
  1493	Moreover, it was argued in \cite{CHR} that the filling factor $\nu$ mod 1 specifies the element in $H^2(BU(1),\Uo)$.
  1494	
  1495	In this sense, the formulation given in \cite{CHR} is more general and unifies two lines of generalizations of the LSM theorem, namely to  U(1) and to finite groups and compact connected semisimple groups.
  1496	It would be interesting to look for a mathematically rigorous formulation which covers both these cases simultaneously.
  1497	
  1498	
  1499	\section{A theorem for reflection invariant models with U(1) symmetry}
  1500	\label{app-U(1)}
  1501	We here describe and prove a theorem corresponding to Corollary~\ref{C:ref} or Theorem~\ref{T:ref} obtained by the original method of U(1) twist devised by Lieb, Schultz, and Mattis \cite[Appendix B]{LSM}.
  1502	Let us consider a standard quantum spin chain on $\bbZ$ as in section~\ref{s:intro}.
  1503	We let the spin quantum number at site $x$ be $\bar{S}_x\in\{\frac{1}{2},1,\frac{3}{2},\ldots,\bar{S}_{\rm max}\}$, and assume the symmetry $\bar{S}_x=\bar{S}_{-x}$.
  1504	The spin chain is described by the formal Hamiltonian $H=\sum_{x\in\bbZ}h_x$, where the local Hamiltonian $h_x$ acts nontrivially only on sites $y$ such that $|y-x|\le R$, and satisfies $\snorm{h_x}\le B$.
  1505	We further assume that $h_x$ is U(1) invariant, i.e.,
  1506	\eq
  1507	\exp\bigl[i\theta\sum_{y:|y-x|\le R}S^{(3)}_x\bigr]\,h_x\,\exp\bigl[-i\theta\sum_{y:|y-x|\le R}S^{(3)}_x\bigr]=h_x,
  1508	\lb{hxU(1)}
  1509	\en
  1510	for any $x\in\bbZ$ and $\theta\in[0,2\pi)$.
  1511	We also define a linear $*$-automorphism $\tcR$ by $\tcR(S^{(1)}_x)=S^{(1)}_{-x}$ and $\tcR(S^{(\nu)}_x)=-S^{(\nu)}_{-x}$ for $\nu=2,3$, for all $x\in\bbZ$.
  1512	Note that $\tcR$ describes a $\bbZ_2$ transformation which consists of reflection about the origin and the $\pi$-rotation about the 1-axis.
  1513	We make an essential assumption that $\tcR(h_x)=h_{-x}$ for all $x\in\bbZ$.
  1514	The model has a $\Uo\rtimes\bbZ_2$ symmetry.
  1515	
  1516	Note that the $\bbZ_2$ symmetry in this case is not an on-site symmetry, but is a global symmetry described by $\tcR$.
  1517	This means that the present class of models is not covered by Corollary~\ref{C:ref} or Theorem~\ref{T:ref}.\footnote{%
  1518	As a special case, one can consider spin chains which have both on-site $\Uo\rtimes\bbZ_2$ symmetry and reflection symmetry.
  1519	Corollary~\ref{C:ref} certainly applies to such models because $\ZZ\subset\Uo\rtimes\bbZ_2$.
  1520	}
  1521	
  1522	The main result of the present Appendix is the following.
  1523	\begin{T}\label{T:refU1}
  1524	Suppose that the above quantum spin chain has a unique ground state $\omega$.
  1525	When $\bar{S}_0$ is a half-odd integer, there is no gap above the ground state.
  1526	\end{T}
  1527	
  1528	\noindent\underline{Proof}:
  1529	We follow Affleck and Lieb \cite{AL} and define the local twist operator
  1530	\eq
  1531	U_\ell\coloneqq\exp\Bigl[-i\sum_{x=-\ell}^\ell\frac{x+\ell}{\ell}\pi S^{(3)}_x\Bigr],
  1532	\lb{localtwist}
  1533	\en
  1534	for $\ell=1,2,\ldots$.
  1535	From the standard argument based on the U(1) invariance of $h_x$, we find for any $\ell$ that
  1536	\eq
  1537	\bigl|\omega(U^\dagger_\ell H_LU_\ell)-\omega(H_L)\bigr|\le\frac{C}{\ell},
  1538	\lb{UHU}
  1539	\en
  1540	with a constant $C$, where $H_L=\sum_{x=-L}^Lh_x$ with any $L\ge\ell+R$.
  1541	See, e.g., \cite{TasakiLSM,TasakiBook}.
  1542	We also note that
  1543	\eq
  1544	\tcR(U_\ell)=\exp\Bigl[i\sum_{x=-\ell}^\ell\frac{-x+\ell}{\ell}\pi S^{(3)}_x\Bigr]
  1545	=\exp\Bigl[2\pi i\sum_{x=-\ell}^\ell S^{(3)}_x\Bigr]\,U_\ell

