   257	{\Large\bf Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type theorems for quantum spin chains\\ without continuous symmetry}
   258	\par\bigskip
   259	
   260	\renewcommand{\thefootnote}{\fnsymbol{footnote}}
   261	\noindent
   262	Yoshiko Ogata\footnote{%
   263	Graduate School of Mathematical Sciencesm
   264	The University of Tokyo, Komaba, Tokyo 153-8914, Japan.
   265	Supported in part by
   266	the Grants-in-Aid for
   267	Scientific Research, JSPS.}
   268	and Hal Tasaki\footnote{%
   269	Department of Physics, Gakushuin University, Mejiro, Toshima-ku, 
   270	Tokyo 171-8588, Japan.
   271	}
   272	\renewcommand{\thefootnote}{\arabic{footnote}}
   273	\setcounter{footnote}{0}
   274	
   275	\begin{quotation}
   276	\small\noindent
   277	{\bf Abstract:}
   278	We prove that  a quantum spin chain with half-odd-integral spin cannot have a unique ground state with a gap, provided that the interaction is short ranged, translation invariant, and possesses time-reversal symmetry or $\ZZ$ symmetry (i.e., the symmetry with respect to the $\pi$ rotations of spins about the three orthogonal axes).
   279	The proof is based on the deep analogy between the matrix product state formulation and the representation of the Cuntz algebra in the von Neumann algebra $\pi(\caA_{R})''$ constructed from the ground state restricted to the right half-infinite chain.
   280	\end{quotation}
   281	
   282	%\tableofcontents
   283	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   284	\section{Introduction}
   285	\label{s:intro}
   286	Quantum spin systems have been active topics of research in both theoretical and mathematical physics.
   287	See, e.g., \cite{BR1,BR2,Sutherland,ZengChenZhouWenBOOK,TasakiBook}.
   288	The Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem \cite{LSM} is one of mathematically rigorous results for quantum spin chains which have had strong and long lasting impact on physics research.
   289	The theorem, in the form extended to infinite chains by Affleck and Lieb \cite{AL}, is a no-go theorem which states that the standard Heisenberg spin chain with half-odd integral spin can never have a unique ground state with a nonvanishing energy gap.
   290	The theorem has been extended to a large class of one-dimensional quantum many-body systems \cite{OYA,YOA,TasakiLSM} and also to systems in higher dimensions \cite{O,H1,H2,NS}.
   291	See \cite{AizenmanNachtergaele1994} for a similar theorem which applies to a different class of quantum spin chains.
   292	
   293	In the original theorem \cite{LSM} and all the extensions mentioned above, an essential assumption is that the spin system has U(1) symmetry, i.e., the invariance with respect the uniform spin rotation about a single axis.
   294	The theorems for one-dimensional systems are proved with a variational state constructed by applying a gradual nonuniform U(1) rotation to the ground state.
   295	The U(1) invariance of the ground state guarantees that the variational state has low excitation energy.
   296	
   297	Recently it has been argued that Lieb-Schultz-Mattis type no-go theorems should be valid for quantum many-body systems which do not necessarily posses continuous symmetry \cite{ChenGuWEn2011,PTAV,WPVZ,Watanabe2018}.
   298	In particular it was conjectured that {\em a quantum spin chain with half-odd-integral spin cannot have a unique ground state with a gap, when the interaction is short ranged, translation invariant, and possesses time-reversal symmetry or $\ZZ$ symmetry}\/ (i.e., the symmetry with respect to the $\pi$ rotations of spins about the three orthogonal axes).
   299	This conjecture was first stated by Chen, Gu, and Wen \cite{ChenGuWEn2011} as (small) parts of their general classification based on the analysis of fixed-point matrix product states.\footnote{%
   300	The case with $\ZZ$ symmetry is treated implicitly in section~V.B.4, and the case with time-reversal symmetry is discussed in section~V.C.}
   301	Later, Watanabe, Po, Vishwanath, and Zaletel \cite{WPVZ} presented a proof that applies to matrix product states (MPS).\footnote{%
   302	They only discuss the case with time-reversal symmetry, but the case with $\ZZ$ symmetry may be treated similarly.
   303	We note that not all of the results in \cite{WPVZ} are mathematically rigorous.
   304	But we expect that the argument in Supporting Information of \cite{WPVZ} which involves [S1--9] can be made rigorous by using standard techniques in matrix product states, provided that one only considers quantum spin chains. 
   305	See also section~8.3.3 of \cite{TasakiBook} for proofs for MPS.
   306	}
   307	They assumed that a unique gapped ground state can be represented as an injective MPS, and examined the projective representation of the symmetry group on the set of matrices defining the MPS.
   308	They found that the assumed symmetry is inconsistent with possible projective representations, thus excluding the existence of an injective MPS.
   309	
   310	In the present paper, we prove the above conjecture and establish Lieb-Mattis-Schultz type no-go theorems for quantum spin chains without continuous symmetry.
   311	See Theorems~\ref{main1} and \ref{main2} for precise statements.
   312	
   313	Our proof is based on the deep correspondence, which was developed in \cite{bjp,BJ,Matsui3,bjkw,Matsui1,Matsui2}, between MPS \cite{FNW} and the Cuntz algebra associated with the translation in the half-infinite chain.
   314	More precisely one can define operators $\cs_\mu$ (with $\mu=-S,-S+1,\ldots,S-1,S$) in the von Neumann algebra $\pi(\caA_{R})''$ constructed from the ground state restricted to the right half-infinite chain, provided that the ground state is pure, translation invariant, and satisfies the condition called the split property.
   315	A remarkable fact, which is essential for us, is that the operators $\cs_\mu$ may be regarded as infinite dimensional analogues of matrices that define a MPS.
   316	Then one can basically repeat the argument for MPS and examine projective representations of the symmetry group on the set of operators $\{\cs_\mu\}$ to prove the theorems.
   317	
   318	As far as we know, the strategy to focus on projective representations (on matrices for MPS) of symmetry groups appeared in physics literature in 2008, in the context of the characterization of symmetry protected topological phases \cite{Perez-Garcia2008,PollmannTurnerBergOshikawa2010}.\footnote{%
   319	It is likely that the idea to make use of projective representations, at least implicitly, can be found in earlier works on quantum spin systems.
   320	}
   321	We should note however that, in 2001, Matsui developed a mathematical theory for quantum spin chains based on projective representations of group symmetry, not only for MPS but for more general pure states which satisfy the split property \cite{Matsui1}.\footnote{%
   322	We also learned that the emergence of projective representations in MPS was already realized by Fannes, Nachtergaele, and Werner back in 1990's, although the work was not published \cite{N}.  (See also the remark at the end of section~1 of \cite{Matsui1}.)}
   323	Matsui then proved an extended version of Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem for a class of quantum spin chains with continuous symmetry.
   324	%By using the projective representation method, Matsui proved an extended version of Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem for a class of quantum spin chains with continuous symmetry \cite{Matsui1}.
   325	In this sense we can regard the present work as a direct generalization of Matsui's work to certain systems without continuous symmetry.
   326	
   327	
   328	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
   329	\section{Setting and main results}
   330	We start by summarizing standard setup of quantum spin chains on the infinite chain \cite{BR1,BR2}.
   331	Let $S$ be an element of $\frac 12 \bbN$ and let $\SSS=\{-S,-S+1,\ldots,S-1,S\}$.
   332	We denote the algebra of $(2S+1)\times (2S+1)$ matrices by $\Mat_{2S+1}$.
   333	We fix an orthonormal basis $\{\psi_\mu\}_{\mu\in\SSS}$ of $\cc^{2S+1}$, and 
   334	set $e_{\mu,\nu}=\ket{\psi_\mu}\bra{\psi_\nu}$ for each $\mu,\nu\in\SSS$.
   335	Let $\hS_1, \hS_2, \hS_3\in\Mat_{2S+1}$ be the standard spin operators specified by $(\hS_1)^2+(\hS_2)^2+(\hS_3)^2=S(S+1)$ and the commutation relations $[\hS_1,\hS_2]=i\hS_3$, $[\hS_2,\hS_3]=i\hS_1$, and $[\hS_3,\hS_1]=i\hS_2$.
   336	
   337	We denote the set of all finite subsets in ${\bbZ}$ by ${\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$.
   338	For each $z\in\bbZ$,  let $\caA_{\{z\}}$ be an isomorphic copy of $\Mat_{2S+1}$, and for any finite subset $\Lambda\subset\bbZ$, let $\caA_{\Lambda} = \otimes_{z\in\Lambda}\caA_{\{z\}}$, which is the local algebra of observables. 
   339	For finite $\Lambda$, the algebra $\caA_{\Lambda} $ can be regarded as the set of all bounded operators acting on
   340	the Hilbert space $\otimes_{z\in\Lambda}{\bbC}^{2S+1}$.
   341	We use this identification freely.
   342	If $\Lambda_1\subset\Lambda_2$, the algebra $\caA_{\Lambda_1}$ is naturally embedded in $\caA_{\Lambda_2}$ by tensoring its elements with the identity. 
   343	The algebra $\caA_{R}$ (resp. $\caA_L$) representing the half-infinite chain
   344	is given as the inductive limit of the algebras $\caA_{\Lambda}$ with $\Lambda\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$, $\Lambda\subset[0,\infty)$
   345	(resp. $\Lambda\subset(-\infty -1]$). 
   346	The algebra $\caA$, representing the two sided infinite chain
   347	is given as the inductive limit of the algebras $\caA_{\Lambda}$ with $\Lambda\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$. 
   348	Note that $\caA_{\Lambda}$ for $\Lambda\in {\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$,
   349	and $\caA_R$ can be regarded naturally as subalgebras of
   350	$\caA$.
   351	Under this identification, for each $z\in \bbZ$,
   352	we denote the 
   353	spin operators in $\caA_{\{z\}}\subset\caA$ by $\hS_{1}^{(z)}, \hS_{2}^{(z)}, \hS_{3}^{(z)}$.
   354	We denote the set of local observables by $\caA_{\rm loc}=\bigcup_{\Lambda\in{\mathfrak S}_\bbZ}\caA_{\Lambda}
   355	$.
   356	We denote by $\tau_x$ the automorphisms on $\caA$ representing the space translation by  $x\in\bbZ$.
   357	
   358	A mathematical model of a quantum spin chain is fully specified by its interaction $\Phi$.
   359	An interaction is a map $\Phi$ from 
   360	${\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$ into ${\caA}_{\rm loc}$ such
   361	that $\Phi(X) \in {\caA}_{X}$ 
   362	and $\Phi(X) = \Phi(X)^*$
   363	for $X \in {\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$. 
   364	An interaction $\Phi$ is translation invariant if
   365	$\Phi(X+x)=\tau_x(\Phi(X))$, 
   366	for all $x\in{\mathbb Z}$ and $X\in  {\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$, and  is of finite range if there exists  $m\in {\mathbb N}$ such that
   367	$\Phi(X)=0$ for $X$ with diameter larger than $m$.
   368	For an interaction $\Phi$ and a finite set $\Lambda\in{\mathfrak S}_{\bbZ}$, we define the local Hamiltonian
   369	as
   370	\begin{equation}\label{GenHamiltonian}
   371	\lmk H_{\Phi}\rmk_{\Lambda}:=\sum_{X\subset{\Lambda}}\Phi(X).
   372	\end{equation}
   373	
   374	Let $\Phi$ be a translation invariant finite range interaction. For each $A\in \caA$ and $t\in{\mathbb R}$, the limit
   375	\begin{equation}
   376	\alpha_t^{\Phi}(A)=\lim_{\Lambda\to\bbZ} e^{it(H_{\Phi})_{\Lambda}} Ae^{-it(H_{\Phi})_{\Lambda}}
   377	\end{equation}
   378	exists and defines a strongly continuous one parameter group of automorphisms $\alpha^\Phi$ on $\caA$. 
   379	(See \cite{BR2}.)
   380	We denote the generator of $C^* $-dynamics $\alpha^{\Phi}$ by $\delta_{\Phi}$.
   381	
   382	A state $\omega$ on $\caA$ is called an \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} state
   383	if the inequality
   384	$
   385	-i\,\omega(A^*{\delta_{\Phi}}(A))\ge 0
   386	$
   387	holds
   388	for any element $A$ in the domain $\caD({\delta_{\Phi}})$ of ${\delta_\Phi}$.
   389	Let $\omega$ be an $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state, with the GNS triple $(\caH_\omega,\pi_\omega,\Omega_\omega)$.
   390	Then there exists a unique positive operator $H_{\omega,\Phi}$ on $\caH_\omega$ such that
   391	$e^{itH_{\omega,\Phi}}\pi(A)\Omega_\omega=\pi_\omega(\alpha_t^\Phi(A))\Omega_\omega$,
   392	for all $A\in\caA$ and $t\in\mathbb R$.
   393	We call this $H_{\omega,\Phi}$ the bulk Hamiltonian associated with $\omega$.
   394	Note that $\Omega_\omega$ is an eigenvector of $H_{\omega,\Phi}$ with eigenvalue $0$.
   395	
   396	The following definition clarifies what we mean by a model with a unique gapped ground state.
   397	See \cite{BR2} for the general theory.
   398	\begin{defn}
   399	We say that a model with an interaction $\Phi$ has a unique gapped ground state if 
   400	(i)~the $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state, which we denote as $\varphi$, is unique, and 
   401	(ii)~there exists $\gamma>0$ such that
   402	$\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})\setminus\{0\}\subset [\gamma,\infty)$, where  $\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})$ is the spectrum of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$.
   403	\end{defn}
   404	Note that the uniqueness of $\varphi$ implies that 0 is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$.
   405	
   406	We now introduce two types of symmetry relevant to the present study.
   407	Time-reversal is the unique  antilinear unital \mbox{$*$-automorphism} $\Xi$ on $\caA$ satisfying
   408	\begin{align}
   409	\Xi(S_j^{z}) = -S_j^{(z)},\quad j=1,2,3,\quad z\in\bbZ.
   410	\end{align}
   411	(See Appendix \ref{TR} for the existence of such an automorphism.)
   412	For a state $\omega$ on $\caA$, its time-reversal $\hat \omega$ is
   413	given by 
   414	\begin{equation}
   415	\hat \omega(A)
   416	=\omega(\Xi(A^*)),\quad
   417	A\in \caA.
   418	\end{equation}
   419	We say $\omega$ is time-reversal invariant if $\omega=\hat\omega$.
   420	
   421	We also introduce the $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$ symmetry.
   422	Let $R_j$ with $j=1,2,3$ be the unique set of unital \mbox{$*$-automorphisms} on $\caA$ satisfying
   423	\begin{equation}
   424	R_j\bigl(\hS_k^{(z)}\bigr)  
   425	=
   426	\left\{
   427	\begin{gathered}
   428	\hS_k^{(z)},\quad j=k\\
   429	-\hS_{k}^{(z)},\quad j\neq k.
   430	\end{gathered} 
   431	\right.,\quad \quad j,k=1,2,3,
   432	\end{equation}
   433	for each $z\in \bbZ$.
   434	Note that $R_j$ is essentially the global spin rotation by $\pi$ about the $j$-axis.
   435	
   436	An interaction $\Phi$ is said to be time-reversal invariant
   437	if $\Xi(\Phi(X))=\Phi(X)$
   438	for all $X\in {\mathfrak S}_\bbZ$.
   439	If $\omega$ is an \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} state of a translation and time-reversal invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$,
   440	then
   441	$\hat \omega$ and $\omega\circ\tau_x$ for any $x\in\bbZ$ are also \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} states.
   442	In particular, if $\omega$ is a unique \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} state, it is pure, time-reversal invariant,  
   443	and translation invariant.
   444	Similarly, 
   445	an interaction $\Phi$ is $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$ invariant if
   446	 $R_j(\Phi(X))=\Phi(X)$ for $j=1,2$ and
   447	for all $X\in {\mathfrak S}_\bbZ$.
   448	Note that $\Phi(X)$ automatically satisfies $R_3(\Phi(X))=\Phi(X)$ .
   449	If $\omega$ is an \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} state of a translation and $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$ symmetry invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$,
   450	then
   451	$\omega\circ R_j$ for $j=1,2,3$ and $\omega\circ\tau_x$ for $x\in\bbZ$ are also \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} states.
   452	In particular, if $\omega$ is a unique \mbox{$\alpha^{\Phi}$-ground} state, it is pure,  $\ZZ$ invariant, and translation invariant.
   453	
   454	
   455	We are ready to state our main theorems.
   456	\begin{thm}\label{main1}
   457	Suppose that $S$ is a half-odd-integer.
   458	Then a model with a translation and time-reversal invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$ does not have a unique gapped ground state.
   459	\end{thm}
   460	\begin{thm}\label{main2}
   461	Suppose that $S$ is a half-odd-integer.
   462	Then a model with a translation and $\bbZ_2\times\bbZ_2$ invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$ does not have  a unique gapped ground state.
   463	\end{thm}
   464	
   465	The theorems are  proven in Sections~\ref{main1p} and \ref{main2p}.
   466	
   467	A typical interaction $\Phi$ with both time-reversal and $\ZZ$ symmetry, to which either Theorem~\ref{main1} or \ref{main2} apply, is that of the XYZ model,
   468	\begin{equation}
   469	\Phi(\{x,y\})=\sum_{j=1}^3J_j(x-y)\,S^{(x)}_jS^{(y)}_j,
   470	\end{equation}
   471	with $J_j(z)\in\bbR$ such that $J_z(z)=0$ if $|z|\ge m$ for some $m\in\bbN$. 
   472	Note that the original Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem \cite{LSM,AL} applies only to the case with $J_1(z)=J_2(z)$.
   473	An interaction with time-reversal symmetry but not necessarily with $\ZZ$ symmetry is
   474	\begin{equation}
   475	\Phi(\{x,y\})=\sum_{j,j'=1}^3J_j(x-y)\,S^{(x)}_jS^{(y)}_{j'},
   476	\end{equation}
   477	and an interaction with $\ZZ$ symmetry but not with  time-reversal symmetry is
   478	\begin{equation}
   479	\Phi(\{x,y,z\})=J(x-z,y-z)\,S^{(x)}_1S^{(y)}_2S^{(z)}_3.
   480	\end{equation}
   481	
   482	\bigskip
   483	\noindent
   484	{\em Remarks:}
   485	1. We in fact prove a more general statement that there can be no  translation invariant pure states with the split property which have either time-reversal or $\ZZ$ symmetry.
   486	But the unique gapped ground states seem to be the only physically meaningful examples.
   487	
   488	2. We can relax the assumption of translation invariance.
   489	The only property we need for the theorems is that the translation on the spin chain induces a unital endomorphism on  $\pi(\caA_{R})''$.
   490	(See Theorem~\ref{cun}.)
   491	This means that the interactions need not to be strictly translation invariant.
   492	It suffices if the interaction satisfies the following boundedness condition
   493	\begin{equation}
   494	\sup_{x\in\bbZ}\sum_{X\ni x}|X|^{-1}\lV \Phi(X)\rV <\infty.
   495	\end{equation}
   496	and the unique ground state is
   497	quasi-equivalent to its space translation by an arbitrary amount.
   498	
   499	3. The requirement that the interaction $\Phi$ has a finite range can be weakened to that of an exponential decay.
   500	
   501	
   502	%%%%%%%%%%%%
   503	\section{The split property}
   504	\label{s:split}
   505	Before going into the proofs, we review an important notion known as the split property in operator algebra.
   506	We in particular recall two results which are  essential for us.
   507	
   508	We here give the following definition of the split property, which is most suitable for our purpose.
   509	It corresponds to the standard definition \cite{dl} in our setting (see \cite{Matsui2}).
   510	See Definition~1.1 of \cite{Matsui2} for a different characterization, which is more physically motivated.
   511	\begin{defn}
   512	Let $\varphi$ be a pure state on $\caA$. Let $\varphi_R$ be the ristriciton of
   513	$\varphi$ to $\caA_R$, and $(\caH,\pi,\Omega)$ be the GNS triple of $\varphi_R$.
   514	We say $\varphi$ satisfies the split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$,
   515	if the von Neumann algebra $\pi(\caA_{R})''$ is a type I factor.
   516	\end{defn}
   517	The important connection between this abstract notion and our physical problem is given by the following result due to Matsui, which is based on Hastings's area law theorem \cite{area}.
   518	\begin{thm}[Corollary 3.2 of \cite{Matsui2}]\label{matsui}
   519	Let $\varphi$ be a (not necessarily unique) pure $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state of a translation invariant finite range interaction $\Phi$, and denote by  $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ the corresponding bulk Hamiltonian. 
   520	Assume that $0$ is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ and
   521	there exists $\gamma>0$ such that $\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})\setminus\{0\}\subset [\gamma,\infty)$.
   522	Then $\varphi$ satisfies the  split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$.
   523	\end{thm}
   524	Recall that a type I factor is isomorphic to $B(\caK)$, the set of all bounded operators 
   525	on a  Hilbert space $\caK$. 
