  2270	\subsection{Split property of unique gapped ground state in $1$-d}
  2271	
  2272	An important property of the unique gapped ground state in one dimension
  2273	is the split property.
  2274	Using this property, one can easily define indices, which turns out to be invariants of the classification.
  2275	More precisely, the following Theorem is crucial in order to define the SPT-index
  2276	in one-dimensional systems. 
  2277	\begin{thm}[Matsui '13]\label{matsuisplit}
  2278	Let $\omega_{\Phi}$ be a {\it  unique gapped $\alpha^\Phi$-ground state}
  2279	for $\Phi\in \caP_{U.G.}$.
  2280	% and denote by  $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ the corresponding bulk Hamiltonian. 
  2281	%Assume that $0$ is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $H_{\varphi,\Phi}$ and
  2282	%there exists $\gamma>0$ such that $\sigma(H_{\varphi,\Phi})\setminus\{0\}\subset [\gamma,\infty)$.
  2283	Then $\omega_{\Phi}$ satisfies the split property with respect to $\caA_L$ and $\caA_R$, i.e.,
  2284	$\pi_{\omega_{\Phi, R}}(\caA_R)''$ is a type I factor.
  2285	\end{thm}
  2286	Thanks to this theorem, known for {\it any} unique gapped ground state, 
  2287	we can classify
  2288	\begin{align*}
  2289	\caP_{U.G.{{\beta}}}:=
  2810	Note that it does not change the 2nd cohomology class associated to
  2811	$u_L$.
  2812	Hence we obtain
  2813	\begin{align}\label{Vuu}
  2814	V_g=u_L(g)\otimes u_R(g),\quad g\in G.
  2815	\end{align}
  2816	From the fact that $V$ is a genuine representation,
  2817	while $u_L$, $u_R$ are projective representations
  2818	with $2$-cocycles $\sigma_L$, $\sigma_R$, we get
  2819	\begin{align*}
  2820	&u_L(gh)\otimes u_R(gh)
  2821	=V_{gh}
  2822	=V_gV_h\\
  2823	&=u_L(g) u_L(h)\otimes u_R(g)u_R(h)
  2824	=\sigma_L(g, h)\sigma_R(g,h)
  2825	u_L(gh)\otimes u_{R}(gh).
  2826	\end{align*}
  2827	Hence we obtain the relation
  2828	\begin{align*}
  2829	\sigma_L(g,h)\sigma_R(g,h)=\unit.
  2830	\end{align*}
  2831	Let us think about what this means.
  2832	First, $V$ is a {\it genuine} representation
  2833	corresponding to the $\beta$-action
  2834	of $G$ on the {\it whole chain}.
  2835	It splits into left and right, to projective representations
  2836	$u_L(g)$ and $u_R(g)$.
  2837	Let us consider the special case that  $\omega_{\Phi}$ is of product form with respect to left-right cut,
  2838	$\omega_{\Phi}=\omega_{\Phi,L}\otimes\omega_{\Phi,R}$.

